{
  "raw": {
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Residential-Life-Coordinator_JR101648": {
      "title": "Residential Life Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-10\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Assistant Director Residential Life Time Type: Full time Compensation: $39,484.62-$49,355.78 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Responsible for the supervision of paraprofessional student staff members. Responsible for facilitating student development through educational programming and the overall management of all aspects of health and safety within multiple residence halls.   Supervises, trains, evaluates and selects Resident Assistant staff. Facilitates the development of a community and educational environment which encourages inclusive and positive relationships among students through social and educational programming.  Provides support and resources to students in individual meetings. Counsels, advises and assists residents concerning personal, interpersonal and facilities concerns. Uses multiple software platforms, including THD, StarRez, Maxient, etc., to track and direct operational components of the residence hall move-in/move-out, student conduct and student wellness processes. Coordinates and implements all aspects of an assigned program area, including first-year, sophomore year and upper-division experience. Acts as a liaison between residents and Facilities Services, ensuring all maintenance and housekeeping requests are completed in a timely manner. Serves as the primary point of contact for the students in their residence halls with a variety of presenting issues, including mental health issues, academic struggles, interpersonal conflicts, etc.  Assists students which requires collaboration with parents and professional staff and faculty in Student Affairs and Academic Affairs. Manages Resident Assistant program budget, through receipt reconciliation and Purchase Card oversight. Enforces and supports Trinity University and Residential Life policies, processes and protocols. Serves on a variety of Student Life committees including CAS Review committees and search committees.  Serves as a Campus Security Authority (CSA), as defined by the Clery Act of 1998.  Serves as Summer Housing Coordinator, as assigned. Reports all relevant details about alleged sexual misconduct involving any student, faculty or staff about which they know or have reason to know. ADDITIONAL DUTIES Serves in the after hours on-call duty rotation for approximately 10 to 13 weeks throughout the calendar year. Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience and integrity to provide professional leadership during emergencies. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Performs other duties as required. EDUCATION Required: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. Preferred: Master's degree or equivalent from an accredited institution. EXPERIENCE Required: One year of experience working in a Residential Life office as an undergraduate, graduate, or professional staff member on a college campus. Preferred: One to two years of professional live-in experience. Demonstrate experience and initiative with student and community development. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Required: Possess a commitment and ability to create communities that are inclusive of diverse populations. Excellent interpersonal, communication and leadership skills. Proven ability to manage crises, resolve conflicts and make sound decisions under pressure. Ability to manage confidential and sensitive information. Effective time management skills."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Vivarium-Technician_JR101641-1": {
      "title": "Vivarium Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: D. R. Semmes School of Science Time Type: Full time Compensation: $18.98-$23.73 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: The Vivarium Technician maintains the animal care facility and provides consistent, high-quality husbandry services in accordance with Trinity University policies and procedures. The position operates and maintains facility equipment and supports the daily care of research animals housed at the institution. The Vivarium Technician is expected to be self-motivated, dependable, attentive to detail, and able to work independently with minimal supervision.   JOB DUTIES Provides daily husbandry for multiple laboratory animal species, including health checks and observations; feeding; monitoring automatic and manual watering systems; changing and cleaning animal cages, racks, and related accessories; and safely handling and restraining animals. Provides animal-room services, including maintaining complete and accurate daily room logs; monitoring expiration dates for consumables; following special husbandry instructions associated with approved research needs; and sanitizing rooms, surfaces, and equipment using appropriate disinfectants and detergents. Operates cage-wash and sanitation equipment. Prepares cages, racks, transport carts, and related equipment for washing by breaking down caging, removing bedding and waste, and dumping or scraping cages as required. Reassembles and prepares clean caging and equipment for use. Stocks and maintains husbandry and facility supplies, including personal protective equipment, animal feed, bedding, enrichment materials, cleaning products, and other consumables. Reports low inventory, equipment malfunctions, and facility concerns promptly. Complies with institutional health and safety requirements, general laboratory practices, approved IACUC protocols and policies, standard operating procedures, and applicable animal-welfare requirements. Participates in required training and promptly reports animal-health, safety, or compliance concerns. ADDITIONAL DUTIES Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience, and integrity. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Performs other duties as assigned. EDUCATION Required: High school diploma or equivalent. Preferred: Bachelor’s degree in biology or animal sciences. EXPERIENCE Required: One year of experience in animal care, laboratory animal husbandry, veterinary care, research support, or a related setting. Preferred: Three years of experience in animal care, laboratory animal husbandry, veterinary care, research support, or a related setting. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Required: Ability to learn and consistently follow animal care, safety, sanitation, and facility procedures. Ability to recognize and promptly report changes in animal health, behavior, food or water consumption, and environmental conditions. Ability to safely handle and restrain multiple laboratory animal species after appropriate training. Time and task management skills, including the ability to complete scheduled care activities reliably and accurately. Organization, recordkeeping, and written and verbal communication skills. Preferred: Knowledge of laboratory animal care practices, biosecurity principles, and IACUC requirements. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS Preferred: AALAS Assistant Laboratory Animal Technician (ALAT) certification, or willingness to pursue certification. OTHER REQUIREMENTS All jobs require a current Criminal Background Check (CBC). Ability to work scheduled weekends, holidays, and emergency or overtime assignments as required to maintain continuous animal care. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES None. DECISION MAKING Performs recurring work under established procedures while exercising judgment to identify and promptly report unusual animal-health, facility, equipment, safety, or research conditions. BUDGET RESPONSIBILITY None. FINANCIAL RESPONSIBILITY None."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Peace-Officer_JR101622-1": {
      "title": "Police Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-09\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Sergeant of Patrol Time Type: Full time Compensation: $23.97-$29.96 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Trinity University Police Department Full-Time | Trinity University Campus | Starting Pay: $28.85/hour The Trinity University Police Department is seeking a TCOLE-certified Police Officer to serve and protect the Trinity University community. As a Trinity University Police Officer, you will help provide a safe and welcoming campus environment for students, faculty, staff, and visitors. Officers are expected to exercise sound judgment, professionalism, integrity, and respect while enforcing applicable laws and University policies. This role requires an officer who understands that effective campus policing is about more than enforcement. It requires building relationships, providing service, communicating effectively, responding appropriately during emergencies, and treating every member of the community with dignity and respect.   Starting Pay: $28.85 per hour IMPORTANT: Applicants must currently hold a Texas Commission on Law Enforcement (TCOLE) Peace Officer license or must be TCOLE certified before their first day of employment. What You’ll Do As a Police Officer with Trinity University, you will: Patrol the Trinity University campus by vehicle, bicycle, and on foot. Respond to calls for service and provide assistance to students, employees, and visitors. Conduct preliminary and follow-up investigations involving criminal and non-criminal incidents occurring on University property. Preserve the peace and protect life and property using lawful, professional, and appropriate policing practices. Enforce applicable federal, state, and local laws, including traffic laws and regulations. Conduct traffic stops and issue warnings or citations when appropriate. Make arrests and take individuals into custody when circumstances and applicable law require. Help ensure University buildings, facilities, and property remain safe and secure. Prepare clear, accurate, and timely police reports and other required documentation. Provide information, assistance, and directions to members of the University community and campus visitors. Respond quickly and exercise sound judgment during emergency and high-pressure situations. Work collaboratively with fellow officers, University departments, and external law enforcement and emergency response partners. Follow Trinity University policies, departmental procedures, and applicable laws and regulations. Perform other related duties as assigned. What We’re Looking For Successful Trinity University Police Officers demonstrate: Professionalism, integrity, and sound judgment. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. The ability to remain composed and make rational decisions during emergencies and stressful situations. A commitment to treating individuals with dignity and respect. Strong observation, documentation, and report-writing skills. The ability to interpret and appropriately apply laws, policies, procedures, and departmental directives. A service-oriented approach to working within a diverse university community. The ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a team. Required Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent. Current TCOLE Peace Officer license/certification or must be TCOLE certified before their first day of employment. Valid Texas driver’s license and an acceptable driving record. Ability to meet all applicable requirements for employment as a Texas peace officer. Ability to successfully complete Trinity University’s required pre-employment screening and background investigation. Preferred Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in Criminal Justice or a related field. Two or more years of experience in law enforcement, security, criminal justice, or a related field. Previous experience working in a campus, community-oriented, or service-focused policing environment. Schedule This is a full-time, 40-hour-per-week position. Police Officers must be available to work rotating shifts, weekends, holidays, overtime, and emergency assignments as required. Because the Trinity University Police Department provides continuous campus coverage, schedules may vary based on departmental and University needs. Physical and Working Requirements Police Officers must be able to perform the essential physical responsibilities associated with law enforcement duties, including: Walking and running Standing and sitting for extended periods Climbing stairs Jumping Lifting or moving objects weighing 50 pounds or more Riding a bicycle Operating a motor vehicle Working outdoors and in inclement weather Responding to emergency and potentially hazardous situations Officers may be required to use or operate law enforcement and communications equipment associated with their duties, including authorized firearms, intermediate weapons, radios, vehicles, and other departmental equipment. Pre-Employment Requirements Employment is contingent upon successfully completing all required pre-employment processes, which may include: Comprehensive background investigation Physical examination Drug screening Psychological evaluation Driving-record review Verification of TCOLE licensing and eligibility Other screenings or requirements applicable to employment as a Texas peace officer Applicants must meet applicable legal and licensing standards regarding criminal history and eligibility to serve as a peace officer. Supervision Work is performed under the direction of departmental supervisory personnel and is reviewed through observation, reports, performance standards, and other established departmental practices. Attendance Regular and reliable attendance is an essential function of this position. Officers are expected to work their assigned schedules and any required scheduled or emergency overtime. Serve. Protect. Be Part of the Community. At Trinity University, policing is both a public safety responsibility and a community partnership. Our officers have the opportunity to know the community they serve, build relationships across campus, and contribute directly to an environment where students, employees, and visitors can learn, work, and thrive safely. If you are a TCOLE-certified peace officer or in the process of completing your TCOLE certification who values professionalism, service, accountability, and community engagement, we encourage you to apply. Starting Pay: $28.85 per hour | Full-Time | Required TCOLE certified before their first day of employment."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Classical-Studies---Greek_JR101647": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Classical Studies - Greek",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-08\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Classical Studies Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Classical Studies at Trinity University invites applications for a tenure-track position in Classical Studies, beginning in August 2027, at the rank of Assistant Professor. The department is seeking a dynamic and innovative teacher with an active research profile who has a demonstrated ability in and enthusiasm for teaching ancient Greek language and literature at all levels and Latin up to the intermediate level and who specializes in the ancient Greek world, broadly defined. Particular areas of research and teaching that are attractive to us include Greek history, culture, and literature; multicultural approaches to the ancient Mediterranean world; ancient and modern reception of Classical texts and culture; or digital humanities. Candidates must demonstrate a strong research profile and a firm commitment to teaching at the undergraduate level. The successful candidate will be expected to teach introductory courses while developing upper-division courses in their area of specialization. The ideal candidate will have a facility for interdisciplinary teaching and interest in mentoring students in undergraduate research projects, including in the summer. All faculty are expected to contribute to Pathways, the University’s interdisciplinary general education program.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . The Carlos Alvarez School of Arts and Humanities, endowed with a gift of $25 million , is at the center of Trinity's liberal arts education. Faculty are international leaders in their respective fields, creating engaged learning experiences grounded in careful study of tradition, critical thinking, clear communication, and creative expression. Classical Studies at Trinity is a vibrant department with six tenured / tenure-track faculty and strong connections with other departments on campus, including Anthropology, Art History, Philosophy, and Religion. We encourage our students to pursue undergraduate research, often in collaboration with faculty, and we have been active participants in our university’s summer undergraduate research program. The department participates in a faculty-led study abroad summer course in Rome and in a faculty-led archaeological dig in Cyprus . Both the Department of Classical Studies  and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching   Teach Classical Greek at all levels and Latin to the intermediate level. Teach both lower and upper-level courses in the candidate’s area of interest and expertise. Offer courses that complement departmental offerings and contribute to the Pathways general education curriculum, including the First Year Experience, under a (typically) 3-2 teaching load. Advise departmental majors and minors.    Research Maintain a consistent and productive program of scholarship, to include publication of research, presentations of papers at academic conferences, attendance at professional meetings, and participation in professional organizations.    Service Provide appropriate service to the department, the university, and the profession. QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Ph.D. or A.B.D. in Classics or related field. A.B.D. candidates must present evidence that Ph.D. will have been completed and accepted no later than August 2027. Preferred: Teaching experience, as Instructor of Record or Teaching Assistant (or equivalent) in an undergraduate course related to ancient Greek, Latin, or Classics. EXPERIENCE Preferred: The ideal candidate will have a facility for interdisciplinary teaching and interest in mentoring students in undergraduate research projects, including in the summer. ATTENDANCE All teaching for this position will be conducted in person.  HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants , you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  File 1: A cover letter File 2: A curriculum vitae File 3:  A teaching portfolio, including (i) a statement of teaching philosophy that addresses your teaching interests and abilities; (ii) sample syllabi; and (iii) teaching evaluations (if available) File 4: A writing sample File 5: Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. In addition, please arrange that three letters of recommendation be sent to classics@trinity.edu .  Review of applications will begin on November   3,   2026. CONTACT INFORMATION Nicolle Hirschfeld nhirschf@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Biology---Neurobiology_JR101632": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biology - Neurobiology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-25\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Biology Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Biology at Trinity University is excited to invite applications for a tenure-track assistant professor in Neurobiology beginning August 2027. This position will also support the interdisciplinary Neuroscience major. Candidates must have a Ph.D. (or equivalent terminal degree) in neurobiology, neuroscience, or biology with a concentration in neuroscience (or a related field). Postdoctoral research experience (or equivalent) is preferred. Teaching responsibilities will include Neurobiology and contributing to labs and/or lectures in the Biology core sequence. Neurobiology, the final core course for the Neuroscience major and a Biology upper division elective, is team-taught as an integrated lecture and lab course focused on cellular and molecular understanding of the nervous system using structural and functional experimental approaches. The Biology core sequence consists of three semesters of lecture-lab combinations which are team-taught. Additional teaching opportunities are available in upper division Biology/Neuroscience electives of the candidate’s choosing and general courses in the University curriculum. The candidate is expected to build a sustainable neuroscience research program actively involving undergraduates in obtaining, presenting, and publishing data. Our current faculty employ model systems using lizards, birds, rodents, and non-human primates with emphasis in behavioral research. We therefore aim to hire a faculty member who will broaden experiences available to students in the areas of cellular and molecular research using aquatic vertebrate (e.g. Zebrafish, Xenopus, etc.), invertebrate, cellular, or computational model systems. This research program is core to the mission of the University to provide experiential learning. The candidate’s research and teaching goals should align and demonstrate a commitment to the teacher-scholar model, excelling in both. The candidate must also demonstrate an ability to positively interact with and contribute to the Department and University.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . Teaching excellence is promoted through the Collaborative for Learning and Teaching, which provides pedagogical and professional development opportunities to all disciplines. Research excellence is supported by a generous startup package, equipment and facilities often found at R1 institutions, such as a confocal imaging center, a pre-tenure one semester leave (provided three years teaching and successful reappointment) and eager and capable undergraduate students. Additionally, departments are rebalancing faculty time from a 9/9-contact hours per year, to 6/9 contact hours per year, with opportunity for a course reduction stemming from acquisition of a national grant. Lecture and laboratory teaching are equally weighted as teaching time. While many students work in the lab during the semester, there is a robust and well-supported, 10-week undergraduate summer research program employing over 200 undergraduates across campus. All biology faculty are expected to contribute, with summer compensation secured by the faculty member through a combination of internal and external sources. The Department of Biology is one of eight departments in the D. R. Semmes School of Science, endowed with a gift of $26 million that in part supports student research and faculty development. The D. R. Semmes School of Science is housed in the Center for the Sciences and Innovation , an innovative and collaborative hub for interdisciplinary research and teaching. The Department of Biology serves our majors as well as a wide range of other majors across the university, to include Neuroscience, Biochemistry/Molecular Biology (BCMB, ASBMB-accredited), Chemistry/Biochemistry (ACS-certified), Psychology, Environmental Studies and a large population of pre-health professional students. We are unified in our commitment to a teacher-scholar model and provide high quality education and active undergraduate research and mentorship programs. Tenured and tenure-track faculty in the Department regularly secure competitive externally funded grants and maintain research agendas with national and international visibility. Our laboratories and facilities are stocked with state-of-the art instrumentation and we maintain close ties/user agreements with local universities and research institutions for equipment not housed in the department. In addition, the University and School are committed to supporting faculty in their aspirations to maintain and expand their research interests and agendas. Further information about our department, its faculty and our facilities is available at https://www.trinity.edu/academics/departments/biology In particular, this position will contribute to the Neuroscience Program. The faculty of the Neuroscience Program, an interdisciplinary committee with members from Biology, Psychology, Chemistry, Physics, and Engineering Sciences, have active research programs with undergraduate students. Further information can be found at https://www.trinity.edu/academics/departments/neuroscience-bs Both the Department of Biology and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching   Successful candidates will be expected to teach 3 lecture and/or laboratory courses per semester. These courses will include Neurobiology, other upper and lower division electives, and other courses related to broader University initiatives. Additionally, the successful candidate will be expected to teach in the core curriculum.  Research Successful candidates will be expected to develop a sustainable research program involving undergraduate students in any field of neurobiology or neuroscience, a highly interdisciplinary science, though model systems are restricted to aquatic vertebrate, invertebrate, cellular, or computational model systems.  Service   Successful candidates will be expected to advise departmental majors and engage in other departmental service. The candidate will also become an active member of the Neuroscience steering committee, contributing to curricular discussions, advising majors, and engaging in service to the major. Other service opportunities to the University and the profession is expected as the candidate approaches tenure. APPOINTMENT DETAILS This position will be a 9-month appointment (9 or 12 month disbursement) starting August 2027, with a standard pre-tenure probationary period. QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Ph.D. or similar terminal degree in Neuroscience, Neurobiology, or Biology with a Neuroscience concentration. EXPERIENCE Preferred: Postdoctoral or equivalent experience is highly preferred. KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES A thorough grasp of cellular and molecular neurobiology. ATTENDANCE Maintaining and satisfying minimum on-site attendance (classroom and office hours attendance) is an essential function of this position. All employees in this position are required to satisfy this requirement. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants , you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  File 1: Cover letter File 2: CV with at least 3 references with contact information listed at the end. These will be contacted if you advance to the interview stage. File 3: Append the teaching statement and research statement into one file. Teaching statement should highlight your philosophy of teaching and discuss your experience. Please also address your approach to teaching Neurobiology, and which of the Biology core curriculum you would most like to teach and why. (2 pages or less) Research statement needs to highlight your previous experience, and a plan to establish an independent lab incorporating undergraduates and with external funding. The plan should include ideas to establish your lab with thoughts towards continuing research past tenure. You might consider projects or pieces of projects done by undergraduates working during the semester, 10-weeks during the summer, and/or a thesis. Your model system should be apparent in this plan. (No more than 5 pages) File 4: Unofficial transcripts from Undergraduate and Graduate schools, appended into one file. File 5: Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. Review of applications will begin October 1, 2026. The position remains open until filled. CONTACT INFORMATION Neurobiology Search Chair Gerard Beaudoin, III gerard.beaudoin@trinity.edu   OR Neurobiology Search Administrator Crystal Ramos cramos@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Housing-Assignments-Manager_JR101640": {
      "title": "Housing Assignments Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Housing Operations Time Type: Full time Compensation: $47,026.82-$58,783.52 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Housing Assignments Manager oversees all housing assignments and room change processes to ensure accurate placements. This role oversees the housing lifecycle from application through assignment, ensuring optimal occupancy and a seamless student experience. Directs room reservation process for returning students, and assigns new, first-year, and transfer students. Manages online student housing portal system. Analyzes and facilitates solutions regarding all housing assignment questions. Analyzes and maintains occupancy records in furtherance of meeting the established targeted occupancy rate. Forecasts housing occupancy numbers for institutional budget planning purposes.   JOB DUTIES Coordinates and facilitates all aspects of first-year, transfer, and returning, and summer student  housing assignments including initial placements, waitlists and room changes.  Directs the room reservation process for returning students. Provides clear and accurate communication to all students regarding room reservation timelines, housing options, and requirements.  Serves as the primary point of contact for Student Accessibility Services. Oversees the assignments process and resource allocation for all housing accommodations.   Leads and implements the housing process for emotional support animals (ESAs) and service animals.    Serves as the primary administrator for  StarRez .  Manages Residential Life, Resident Assistant, and relevant campus partner access, develops reports, trains staff on usage, etc. ,    Configures housing application workflows, assignment processes, and reporting tools.  Ensures system configurations align with operational goals and assignment strategies.  Oversees data integrity, reporting accuracy, and system functionality.   Partners with ITS and vendors on system enhancements and troubleshooting.  Serves as the primary point of contact with Student Financial Services. Uploads and manages data for student room charges and meal plans. Responsible for ensuring all data uploads occur in a timely and accurate manner.  Responsible for ensuring the Workday to StarRez imports and exports are thorough and accurate.  Supports meal plan change requests.   Develops, maintains, and communicates occupancy records, reports, and billing .  Prepares detailed reports, including the Residence Hall Occupancy report, that are consumed by the Vice Presidents for Finance and Administration, Student Affairs, and Enrollment Management. Forecasts housing occupancy numbers for institutional budget planning purposes. Coordinates semester verification rosters to ensure all residency-required students are assigned, and that all students with an assignment are registered for twelve (12) credit hours.   Trains staff on housing operations procedures (ex. Room Change; Damage Billing processes).  Manages End of Semester Damage Billing process.  Manages billing and charges for early arrival, Winter Break, and late departures.  Manages ongoing student housing assignment needs throughout the year. Communicates with students, families, and staff regarding housing assignments with care, compassion, and patience, and provides information on housing options and availability. Manages all aspects of the Early Arrival, Winter Break, and Late Departure processes and associated billing. Manages room change requests, cancellations, and ensures compliance with policies and procedures. Facilitates resolutions regarding all housing assignment questions, reservations, residency, conflicts etc.     ADDITIONAL DUTIES Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience and integrity to provide professional leadership during emergencies. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Performs other duties as required. EDUCATION Required: Bachelor's degree from four-year college or university. Preferred: Master’s degree EXPERIENCE Required: Four years of related experience Preferred: Previous experience working within a college student Housing program or student life is strongly preferred KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Required: Knowledge of diverse student populations and the impact those diverse perspectives and identities have on housing needs. Advanced knowledge of StarRez. Familiarity with ADA and Fair Housing Act compliance in a residence hall setting. Knowledge and practice of organizational and time management skills, including ability to handle multiple assignments under pressure. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite and Google Suite, particularly Excel and Google Sheets. Excellent communication skills and ability to interact with students, parents, faculty, and staff. Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive material with a high degree of discretion as well as abiding by FERPA guidelines. Ability to interpret and follow complex oral and written instructions as well as analyze information and make sound decisions. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS None OTHER REQUIREMENTS All jobs require a current Criminal Background Check (CBC). SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES None DECISION MAKING Work where procedures are of varied or diversified nature. BUDGET RESPONSIBILITY Monitors budget activities but no responsibility for budget and expenditure authority. FINANCIAL RESPONSIBILITY None"
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Building-Automation-Systems-Tech_JR101630": {
      "title": "Building Automation Systems Tech",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Assistant Director for Buildings & Grounds Time Type: Full time Compensation: $26.93-$33.66 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Responds to service requests, emergency service calls, expected to maintain positive customer relationships at all times, and has an in-depth knowledge of the ALC platform.   JOB DUTIES Troubleshoots and repairs building automation system field cabinets, including communications equipment, printed circuit boards, power supplies, electrical/electronic controls, sensors, and transducers. Troubleshoots, repairs, or coordinates repair of building automation equipment. Writes and debugs computer programs for the building automation system such as energy consumption reports and utility distribution reports. Coordinates university and contractor’s personnel for additions and modifications of the building automation system, as needed. Monitors work in progress and job costs on building automation systems. Assists in the design and implementation of various building automation hardware and computer software systems. Prepares drawings and diagrams of system additions or revisions after installation. EDUCATION  Required: High School Diploma or GED. Preferred: Formal OJT or Apprentice Program. Completion of a two year vocational training program or apprenticeship training program in related HVAC field. EXPERIENCE  Required: Minimum 10 years field experience in the HVAC controls industry including DDC systems commissioning, software integration operation, calibration and experience with pneumatic controls. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES  Required: Ability to handle multiple priorities. Preferred: Advanced knowledge of Automated Logic commissioning tools and field circuit troubleshooting skills.  Understanding of the construction industry with emphasis in mechanical/electrical fields. Ability to read and understand mechanical and electrical drawings. Strong communication, organizational, documentation, and problem solving skills. Competency in Networking TCP/IP, MSTP protocols, within Modbus, LON, and BACnet applications. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS  Required: Must possess a valid Texas Driver’s License and have a good driving history.  May not have more than two moving violations in a three-year period."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Plumber_JR101631": {
      "title": "Plumber",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-10\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Assistant Director for Buildings & Grounds Time Type: Full time Compensation: $21.33-$26.66 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Performs skilled work in the installation, maintenance, troubleshooting, repair, and optimization of residential and commercial plumbing systems and associated building systems to support safe, reliable, and efficient facility operations. Supports regulatory compliance through backflow prevention testing, certification, program administration, and effective use of work management systems.   JOB DUTIES Performs installation, maintenance, troubleshooting, diagnosis, and repair of residential and commercial plumbing systems including domestic water systems, sanitary sewer systems, storm drainage systems, piping systems, fixtures, valves, pumps, and related plumbing equipment. Performs preventive and predictive maintenance activities on plumbing systems and equipment in accordance with manufacturer recommendations, maintenance plans, and departmental standards. Performs testing, inspection, certification, maintenance, and repair of backflow prevention devices and supports the administration of the University’s backflow prevention program, including regulatory reporting and compliance activities. Troubleshoots and supports plumbing-related controls, pumps, sensors, monitoring systems, and associated building systems to maintain safe and reliable operation. Documents work performed, labor, materials, asset information, inspection records, and testing and certification activities within the work management/CMMS system; assists with identifying recurring issues, supporting regulatory compliance, recommending corrective actions, and planning or identifying material requirements for assigned work activities. Provides customer service and emergency response support, complies with applicable safety procedures and regulatory requirements, and participates in an on-call rotation as assigned. ADDITIONAL DUTIES Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality to support safe, reliable, and customer-focused facility operations. Supports departmental initiatives related to facility asset management, continuous improvement, and service quality. Assists in training and providing guidance to lower-level employees and shares technical knowledge with team members as appropriate. Supports the administration and continuous improvement of plumbing-related compliance programs and asset records, as assigned. Maintains compliance with University policies, safety procedures, and applicable regulatory requirements. Performs other duties as assigned. EDUCATION Required: High School Diploma or GED. Preferred: Completion of a vocational training program, technical certification program, apprenticeship, or equivalent formal training in plumbing, mechanical systems, or a related field. EXPERIENCE Required: Five years of progressively responsible experience in the installation, maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair of residential and/or commercial plumbing systems. Experience with plumbing systems including domestic water systems, sanitary sewer systems, storm drainage systems, piping systems, fixtures, valves, pumps, and related plumbing equipment. Experience performing testing, inspection, maintenance, and repair of backflow prevention devices. Preferred: Experience with commercial plumbing systems in an institutional, higher education, healthcare, or large campus environment. Experience administering or supporting a backflow prevention or cross-connection control program. Experience using computerized work management/CMMS systems to document work activities and maintain records. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Required: Knowledge of residential and commercial plumbing systems including domestic water systems, sanitary sewer systems, storm drainage systems, piping systems, fixtures, valves, pumps, and associated mechanical components. Skill in troubleshooting, diagnosing, maintaining, and repairing plumbing systems and equipment. Knowledge of applicable plumbing codes, cross-connection control principles, and backflow prevention devices and testing requirements. Ability to perform testing, inspection, maintenance, and certification activities for backflow prevention devices. Ability to read and interpret blueprints, schematics, technical manuals, and plumbing system drawings. Ability to follow applicable safety procedures including lockout/tagout, PPE requirements, and safe work practices. Ability to use a computerized work management/CMMS system to document work activities and maintain records. Ability to communicate effectively and provide quality customer service while working independently and as part of a team. Preferred: Ability to prioritize multiple assignments and adapt to changing operational needs. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS Required: Must possess a valid Texas Driver’s License and maintain a driving record acceptable to the University. Must possess a current Texas Journeyman Plumber or Master Plumber license. Must possess or obtain within six months of hire a Texas Backflow Prevention Assembly Tester (BPAT) license/certification. Preferred: Texas Master Plumber license. Specialized training or certifications related to plumbing systems, cross-connection control, pumps, water systems, or related building systems. Training or certifications related to computerized work management/CMMS systems. OTHER REQUIREMENTS All jobs require a current Criminal Background Check (CBC). Rotating shift work is required. Must pass a pre-employment drug screening and physical examination. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES No supervisory responsibility. May provide guidance to level I employees. NUMBER OF DIRECT REPORTS None NUMBER OF INDIRECT REPORTS None DECISION MAKING Work where procedures are of varied or diversified nature. BUDGET RESPONSIBILITY No responsibility for budget, revenues, and/or expenditure authority. FINANCIAL RESPONSIBILITY None"
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Music---Music---Organist_JR101620-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Music - Music Theory & Organist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-07\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Music Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: TRINITY UNIVERSITY, DEPARTMENT OF MUSIC is seeking a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Music and University Organist, beginning August 2027. The Department of Music invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor to serve as a vital link between our academic core and our institutional heritage. We seek a scholar capable of delivering rigorous instruction in the undergraduate music theory sequence while serving as the University Organist. The successful candidate will not only teach and perform but also actively recruit students to maintain a vibrant organ and harpsichord studio. The ability to foster high-level creative activity and research in solo and collaborative performance is essential. This position plays a pivotal role in the public face of the University through ceremonial leadership, the stewardship of the historic Parker Chapel organs, and contributing to the University’s general curriculum and liberal arts mission.   T rinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . The Carlos Alvarez School of Arts and Humanities, endowed with a gift of $25 million , is at the center of Trinity's liberal arts education. Faculty are international leaders in their respective fields, creating engaged learning experiences grounded in careful study of tradition, critical thinking, clear communication, and creative expression. The Music Department operates within the context and mission of Trinity’s commitment to an undergraduate education based on the foundation of a Liberal Arts and Sciences curriculum. The Music Department’s instructional staff consists of 11 full-time tenure-track faculty and a full component of part-time faculty covering private studio lessons and adjunct teaching.  The department offers, in addition to class and performance opportunities to all students irrespective of their major, a minor in music, Bachelor of Arts in Music, and Bachelor of Music degree. Majors may select composition, performance, or pre-certification in music education as a concentration.  Performing ensembles include three curricular choirs, Symphony Orchestra, Symphonic Wind Ensemble, and Jazz Ensemble. Students can also perform in smaller groups such as Handbell Ensemble, Percussion Ensemble, jazz combos, string quartets, and a variety of chamber wind ensembles. Both the Department of Music and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching applied lessons in organ and harpsichord, which includes mentoring students and equipping them with versatile \"portfolio careers\" that combine solo performance, continuo playing, collaborative skills, and worship service leadership. Provide effective instruction within the music theory sequence, which includes courses in written theory, aural skills, and keyboard skills. In partnership with the University Chaplain, provide musical leadership for services taking place in Parker Chapel, such as the annual Vespers service, and serve as the primary musical steward for university-wide ceremonies (including Commencement, Convocation, Baccalaureate). Engage in scholarly and creative activity that leads to peer-reviewed publications, high-profile public performances, conference presentations, or other substantial contributions to the field. Supervise undergraduate research in historical performance practice and the theoretical analysis of both sacred and secular keyboard literature. Advise undergraduate students, supporting their academic progress, recital preparation, and post-graduation success. Oversee and coordinate events that are part of the endowed Parker Chapel Music Series. Partner with the University Chaplain to administer the Brown-Herndon Organ Endowment, providing sustained professional stewardship for the care, preservation, and responsible use of the University's historic pipe organs. A commitment to participate in and contribute to the University’s general curriculum, known as Pathways. Participate in university service, student recruitment, interdisciplinary campus initiatives, and alumni relations efforts. APPOINTMENT DETAILS This position will be a 9-month appointment (9 or 12 month disbursement) starting August 2027, with a standard pre-tenure probationary period. ​   QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Doctoral degree in Music (e.g. DMA, PhD) in Organ Performance, Music Theory, or a closely related field (ABD considered if degree will be conferred before the Fall 2027 term). EXPERIENCE Required: Successful record of providing individual instruction in organ. Demonstrated excellence in teaching within an undergraduate music theory curriculum. Active and established record of high-level solo and/or collaborative organ performance. Preferred: Experience working in a church, cathedral, or university setting, demonstrating capacity for worship planning and ceremonial leadership. Demonstrated professional experience in collaborative keyboard playing, such as accompanying choral ensembles, chamber groups, or continuo playing. A record of successful student recruitment, particularly in engaging non-majors and interdisciplinary students in studio instruction. Experience with organ curation and stewarding historic or endowed instruments. KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES 1. Superior keyboard skills, including service playing, improvisation, solo repertoire, and collaborative performance. 2. Strong foundation in music theory pedagogy, with an emphasis on performance-integrated instruction. 3.Capacity to adapt pedagogical approaches to effectively teach both novice and advanced undergraduate organ/harpsichord students. 4. Ability to provide musical leadership in sacred and ceremonial settings. 5. Willingness to work across departments and interdisciplinary programs. 6. Active scholarly research and creative activity. 7. Openness to curriculum innovation while adapting to evolving educational needs. 8. Active participation in professional organizations. 9. Excellent communication, organization, and time management skills, as well as knowledge of relevant music technologies. 10. Commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and community engagement. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants , you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following 3 file as seen below.    ​​ File 1: Cover Letter File 2: Curriculum vitae, please include list of references and full contact information File 3: Statement of teaching philosophy (1-2 page), including a reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Second round applicants will be asked to provide teaching and performance videos. These may be requested during semester break, so please plan accordingly. ​Complete applications submitted on or before November 20, 2026. The search will remain open until the position has been filled. CONTACT INFORMATION Brian Bondari (bbondari@trinity.edu) Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Air-Conditioning---Heating-Mechanic-II_JR100732": {
      "title": "Air Conditioning & Heating Mechanic II",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-06\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Assistant Director for Buildings & Grounds Time Type: Full time Compensation: $25.40-$31.75 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Performs HVAC mechanical/electrical duties at the factory trained level of proficiency on chilled water systems, commercial VRF systems and secondary HVAC air and water systems.   EDUCATION  Required: High School Diploma or GED. Preferred: Formal OJT or Apprentice Program. Completion of a two-year vocational training program or apprenticeship training program in related HVAC field. EXPERIENCE  Required: Five years of journey level experience in a VRF mechanical and electrical field.   Factory certified training from VRF manufacturer. Preferred: Five years of experience in commercial HVAC mechanical and electrical field. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES  Required: Proficient with commercial DX systems, central plant equipment, and secondary HVAC air and water systems, pumps, heat exchangers. Preferred: Ability to handle multiple priorities. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS  Required: Must possess a valid Texas Driver’s License and have a good driving history.  May not have more than two moving violations in a three-year period. Type III certification for refrigerant recovery/recycling as established by the EPA. Preferred: University certification."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Associate-Director-Alumni-Career--Engagement_JR101627": {
      "title": "Associate Director Alumni Career  Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-04\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Director of Alumni Career Engagement & Network Time Type: Full time Compensation: $52,839.34-$66,049.17 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Reporting to the Director for Alumni Career Engagement, helps fulfill one of the University’s brand promises of helping individuals pursue lives of meaning and purpose, which will be accomplished by engaging alumni through lifelong learning events and resources, professional networks, mentoring, digital media and marketing (webinars and podcasts), and by building a culture in which everyone knows and believes Tigers hire and help Tigers. Ensures that staff collaboratively support the programs and strategies of each of the Alumni Relations and Development departments and the overreaching goals of service to Trinity University students, faculty, donors, alumni and programs.   JOB DUTIES Alumni Learning & Career Development: Directs the creation and implementation of professional development programming and career consultation for alumni, with a focus towards graduates of the last decade and early-career professionals, in accordance with Alumni Career Engagement, Alumni Relations, and University objectives. Introduces and manages the implementation of new and expanded learning and professional development programming targeted towards graduates of last decade and early-career professionals. Programs include but are not limited to Young Alumni Summit, online professional development forums, and regional career clinics. Develops, manages and facilitates production of digital resources and program assets such as the LinkedIn alumni group and ACE web page. Works in collaboration with Advancement Communication to create social media content consisting of career engagement-related topics and current trending topics. Provides 1:1 career development coaching for early and establishing career professionals, focusing on resume and cover letter writing, interview preparation, and salary negotiation. Career exploration guidance provided through self-assessments (such as Strong Interest Inventory interpretations), exploring career changes, and plotting actionable transition plans. Operations & Marketing Management: Assists with the management of the ACE alumni career conversation intake. Reviews, answers, and delegates intake requests, including but not limited to career consultation for alumni. Documents initial request, along with activity reports related to intake conversations. Manages bi-weekly reporting of intake requests. Assists with reporting programmatic outcomes that track alumni engagement. Manages a process to collect and utilize survey, behavioral and other data to improve and refine the program and its offerings. Assists in the development and execution of marketing plans to raise awareness of Alumni Career Engagement offerings, in collaboration with the Office of Advancement and Strategic Communications & Marketing. Volunteer Management: Develops, executes and strengthens pathways for alumni-to-alumni volunteer career guidance opportunities. Works with the Director of Alumni Career Engagement, along with campus partners and faculty to optimize existing programs, while developing new opportunities for alumni to volunteer time and career knowledge through stand-alone connections and development of new, scalable program offerings. Identifies appropriate alumni, faculty and outside experts to speak at programs; coordinates speaker preparation and program materials. Alumni Relations Team Duties: Collaborates with the Alumni Relations team in the development and execution of the Alumni Relations’ signature events such as Alumni Weekend, River Parade, Holiday Party, etc. ADDITIONAL DUTIES Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience and integrity to provide professional leadership during emergencies. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Performs other duties as required. EDUCATION Required: Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution. Preferred: Master's degree from an accredited institution. EXPERIENCE Required: Minimum of two years of paid, internship, and volunteer experience in career consulting/coaching-related or similar work experience in talent management or selection assessment. Minimum of two years experience in program development and management at a higher education institution, a non-profit organization or private business/company.  Preferred: Three years of experience in program development and management at a higher education institution or nonprofit organization. Three years of experience working in an alumni relations program at a higher education institution and/or a nonprofit or for-profit education institution. At least one year of experience producing digital learning resources and social media content.  Three or more years of experience facilitating workshops and leading small group discourse. Budget management experience. Experience producing podcasts and webinars. Familiarity with small liberal arts institutions. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Required: Computer literate with knowledge and ability with word processing equipment and familiarity with data entry and retrieval.  Ability to work non-traditional business hours and weekends. Strong verbal and written communication skills. Detail oriented. Strong management and organizational skills. Ability to provide career coaching consultations in 1:1 and small group settings. Ability to facilitate education workshops and moderate small group discussions. Preferred: Working knowledge of PowerPoint, Canva, Prezi or similar software. Working knowledge of Salesforce and Adobe Premiere Pro. Familiarity with engagement platforms like Anthology, iModules or similar platforms. Project management skills including building and implementing project management plans. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS Required: None Preferred: International Coaching Federation (ICF) OTHER REQUIREMENTS All jobs require a current Criminal Background Check (CBC). Willingness to work some evenings and weekends. Willingness to travel on behalf of Trinity University. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES May assist in supervising student workers and/or volunteers. NUMBER OF DIRECT REPORTS None DECISION MAKING Plans and performs complex work where only general policies or procedures are available. BUDGET RESPONSIBILITY Accountable for rendering counsel and advice regarding the department budget development. FINANCIAL RESPONSIBILITY None"
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Director-of-Aquatics_JR101628": {
      "title": "Director of Aquatics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-03\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Facilities and Event Management Time Type: Full time Compensation: $44,364.92-$55,456.14 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Responsible for operating, staffing, maintaining, and oversight for Hixon Natatorium and the outdoor pool.   JOB DUTIES Directs the daily operation of two aquatics facilities: Hixon Natatorium and outdoor pool.  Supervises and participates in daily pool maintenance for both pools.  Delivers timely and effective customer service to address patron concerns and complaints with aquatics facilities, including locker rooms, and/or events. Hires, trains, schedules, and evaluates student staff lifeguards.  Teaches Aquatic/CPR/AED/First Aid classes for lifeguard staff; PE/Rec Sports/Athletics student staff, and athletic department staff including coaches.  Coordinates event management for intercollegiate swim/dive meets (<5 meets a year).  Performs as substitute lifeguard when necessary for P.E. classes, intercollegiate athletics practices, general recreation open swim, and other aquatic special events. Annually reviews and revises the lifeguard and aquatics operations manuals. Prepares and administers annual aquatics budget for aquatics supplies/expenses, including pool chemicals and lifeguard wages.  Purchases aquatics supplies from local, regional, and national vendors as necessary.  Prepares invoices for lifeguard expenses for off campus user groups as necessary. Directs, coordinates, and evaluates employees.  Carries out supervisory responsibilities in accordance with all policies and applicable laws.  Interviews, hires and trains employees; plans, assigns, and directs work; creates work schedules and conducts performance appraisals, counsels, and disciplines employees; addresses complaints and resolves problems. ADDITIONAL DUTIES  Abides by NCAA Division III rules and regulations. Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience and integrity to provide professional leadership during emergencies. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Performs other duties as required. EDUCATION  Required: Bachelor's degree from four-year college or university. Preferred: Master's degree or equivalent from four-year college or university. EXPERIENCE  Required: 6 months to 12 months. Background in pool management and lifeguard supervision. Experience working with pool mechanical, filtration, and chemical control systems. Experience working for a collegiate, municipal athletic or recreation department. Experience managing various aquatic events and technical knowledge of both Hy-Tek Meet Manager and Colorado Timing Systems products. Management experience working in aquatics, recreation, athletics, physical education or related fields. Preferred: 1 year to 3 years. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES  Required: Knowledge of swim, dive, and other aquatic events. Ability to multi-task while possessing a professional demeanor and attitude. Excellent verbal and written skills. Quality customer service skills. Ability to operate computers and software. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS  Required: American Red Cross (or equivalent), Lifeguarding, first aid, and CPR/AED certification. CPR for Professional Rescuers. American Red Cross (or equivalent), Lifeguard Instructor certification, must be obtained within 30 days of employment. Preferred: American Red Cross Instructor Training certification in CPR/AED/FA. Certified Pool Operator (CPO) from the National Spa and Pool Foundation. Certified Pool Operator (CPO). OTHER REQUIREMENTS All jobs require a current Criminal Background Check (CBC). Comply with and implement all local, state, national, and university safety regulations."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Finance-and-Business-Analytics---Real-Estate--Open-Rank-_JR101613-1": {
      "title": "Calgaard Professor of Real Estate (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-03\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Finance and Business Analytics Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Finance and Business Analytics in the Neidorff School of Business at Trinity University invites applications for a full-time tenure-line faculty position in Finance – Calgaard Professor of Real Estate at the ranks of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor, beginning August 9, 2027. Applicants should hold a Ph.D. or DBA degree in finance or a closely related field, such as Real Estate or Economics, from a AACSB-accredited program. Candidates should have a record of effective teaching in finance and / or real estate courses and scholarly publications in the real estate area. A successful candidate will be expected to deliver high-quality undergraduate courses in finance and real estate. The standard teaching load is five courses per academic year (3-2 teaching load). Each course is based on 3 contact hours per week, with an average class size of 20-25 students. Faculty are expected to maintain an active research agenda and participate in service at the department, school, and university-levels. Trinity faculty have many opportunities to engage in experiential learning, undergraduate research, industry-engaged research, interdisciplinary teaching and research, and faculty-led study abroad programs. As the department continues to build its real estate program, the successful candidate will play a central role in shaping the program’s curriculum, developing industry partnerships, and creating high-impact student learning experiences.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . At the Neidorff School of Business at Trinity University, innovation meets impact. Located in the 7th largest city in the United Sates, where we're redefining what it means to deliver a modern business education with bold ideas, ethical leadership, and global perspective coming together. Our AACSB-accredited programs - ranked #1 among undergraduate business schools in Texas and #27 in the nation - empower students to think strategically, lead confidently, and make ethical decisions grounded in data and purpose. Our faculty are mentors, teacher-scholars, and catalysts for transformation. The Neidorff School thrives on collaborations across disciplines, forging powerful connections with humanities, social sciences and STEM disciplines. With cutting-edge facilities, strong industry partnerships, and a student-centered culture that prized creativity and inclusion, Neidorff is a dynamic hub for shaping the next generation of business leaders who make a difference in the world. Join us, and be a part of a school that's driven by purpose, powered by poeple, and poised for the future. The Department of Finance and Business Analytics offers two undergraduate majors—Finance and Business Analytics—and is home to 13 dedicated faculty committed to teaching excellence and student success. The Finance group, a close-knit team of six faculty supporting about 90 majors, delivers a rigorous and applied curriculum grounded in the liberal arts tradition. Students gain hands-on experience through the Student Managed Fund, Bloomberg Lab, and national competitions that connect classroom learning to real-world finance. The Student Managed Fund is one of the largest undergraduate funds in the nation, currently managing a portfolio of $ 19 million. The department fosters a collaborative, student-centered environment that emphasizes teaching innovation, student career readiness, and the integration of finance and analytics. Both the Department of Finance and Business Analytics and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching and Advising Primarily teach the real estate courses, as well as other finance courses based on departmental needs. Develop and update course materials to ensure relevance, rigor, and alignment with departmental learning goals.  Hold regular office hours and provide academic support to students. Contribute to the design and development of the real estate curriculum as the department continues to build its real estate program. Use active learning, experiential, and technology-enhanced teaching approaches to engage undergraduate students. Create meaningful student learning experiences that connect classroom learning with real estate practice, including applied projects, case studies, site visits, guest speakers, and industry engagement. Support undergraduate research, independent studies, and other student-centered experiential learning opportunities in real estate when appropriate. Provide academic advising to students regarding course selection and progression in the finance curriculum. Advise and mentor students on career paths, internships, and professional certifications in real estate. Research Engage in consistent and productive research and scholarly activities that result in quality publications and other intellectual contributions in the real estate area.  Maintain an active research agenda appropriate to rank. Pursue opportunities for applied, industry-engaged, or interdisciplinary research, including collaborations with real estate firms, professional associations, community partners, and other stakeholders when appropriate.  Support the department’s broader goal of connecting faculty research with student learning, industry engagement, and regional real estate needs. Department, School, and University Service Play a leadership role in building and advancing the department’s real estate program, including curriculum development, industry partnership development, and student experiential learning opportunities. Help cultivate relationships with alumni, employers, professional organizations, and real estate industry partners to support student learning, internships, career opportunities, and program visibility. Serve on committees at the department, school, and university levels appropriate to rank. Participate in departmental and school meetings, especially discussions related to curriculum development and student success. Support departmental initiatives such as major declaration events, open houses, and student recruitment activities, career programing, and alumni engagement. Contribute to creating a collegial, student-centered environment within the Department of Finance and Business Analytics and the Neidorff School of Business. QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Ph.D. or D.B.A. in Finance, Real Estate, Economics, or a related discipline from an AACSB-accredited program. EXPERIENCE Required: Teaching experience in  undergraduate finance courses. A portfolio of research outcomes, demonstrated by a record of quality publications in real estate. Preferred: Demonstrated effectiveness in teaching a range of undergraduate real estate courses, such as real estate finance, real estate investment, real estate development, or real estate analytics. Ability to teach undergraduate finance courses based on departmental needs. Commitment to undergraduate student mentoring, advising, and student success. Commitment to contributing to the department, school and university through service. Experience developing new courses, curriculum, programs, concentrations, or other academic initiatives. Demonstrated use of inclusive, experiential, or technology-enhanced teaching methods that foster engagement among all students. Experience creating high-impact student learning experiences. Professional experience in the real estate industry or experience cultivating or working with industry partners, alumni, professional organizations, or community stakeholders. Ability to contribute to interdisciplinary teaching, research, or experiential learning initiatives across the university. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants , you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  File 1: Cover Lette r – addressing how your skills/abilities align with Trinity University’s mission and with the position requirements. Generic cover letters will invalidate the application. File 2: Curriculum Vitae (CV) with names and email addresses of three references (references may be contacted to submit reference letters electronically).  File 3: Documentation of Teaching Effectiveness – including representative syllabi, list of courses taught, examples of innovative teaching practices, and official original, complete course evaluations with student comments (Items need to be combined in one file). File 4: One or More Research Publications – in the real estate area, as mentioned in job duties and responsibilities (Publications need to be combined into one file). File 5: Intentional Inclusion Statement – Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. CONTACT INFORMATION Dr. Mauro Oliveira (moliveir@trinity.edu), Search Committee Chair  Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Business-Analytics_JR101582": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Business Analytics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-24\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Finance and Business Analytics Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Finance and Business Analytics in the Neidorff School of Business at Trinity University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Business Analytics, beginning in the Fall 2027 semester. Applicants must hold, or be near completion of, a Ph.D. in Business Analytics, Information Systems, Operations Management, Supply Chain, or a closely related field. The successful candidate will be expected to conduct high-quality research, teach and design undergraduate courses in business analytics and supply chain, and provide service to the University, the School, the department, the profession, and the community. Candidates should demonstrate evidence of effectiveness in teaching undergraduate business analytics and supply chain courses, along with an active research pipeline and evidence of scholarly productivity or strong potential for high-quality publications in business analytics, supply chain, or related fields. To support faculty scholarship, Trinity University and the Neidorff School of Business provide research funding, access to databases and technology resources, conference travel support, and pre-tenure academic leave opportunities.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . At the Neidorff School of Business at Trinity University, innovation meets impact. Located in the 7th largest city in the United States, where we’re redefining what it means to deliver a modern business education with bold ideas, ethical leadership, and global perspective coming together. Our AACSB-accredited programs - ranked #1 among undergraduate business schools in Texas and #27 in the nation - empower students to think strategically, lead confidently, and make ethical decisions grounded in data and purpose.   Our faculty are mentors, teacher-scholars, and catalysts for transformation. The Neidorff School thrives on collaboration across disciplines, forging powerful connections with humanities, social sciences and STEM disciplines. With cutting-edge facilities, strong industry partnerships, and a student-centered culture that prizes creativity and inclusion, Neidorff is a dynamic hub for shaping the next generation of business leaders who make a difference in the world.  Join us, and be part of a school that’s driven by purpose, powered by people, and poised for the future. The Department of Finance and Business Analytics offers two undergraduate majors—Finance and Business Analytics—and is home to 13 dedicated faculty committed to teaching excellence and student success. The Business Analytics group, a close-knit team of 7 faculty supporting about 50 majors, delivers a rigorous and applied curriculum grounded in the liberal arts tradition. Students gain hands-on experience through industry sponsored projects and national analytics competitions that connect classroom learning to real-world business analytics. The department fosters a collaborative, student-centered environment that emphasizes teaching innovation, student career readiness, and the integration of finance and analytics.   Both the Department of Finance and Business Analytics and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Conduct high-quality research in business analytics, supply chain, or related fields, that results in publications and other intellectual contributions consistent with the expectations of Neidorff School of Business for an Assistant Professor. Teach and design undergraduate business analytics and supply chain courses. Teaching load is equivalent to five course sections per academic year (i.e., a 2-3 or 3-2 teaching load). Provide service to the University, the Neidorff School of Business, the department, the profession, and the community at a level that is expected for faculty at the same rank. QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Ph.D degree completed or ABD with an expected completion date prior to August 2027 in Business Analytics, Information Systems, Operations Management, Supply Chain, or a related field EXPERIENCE Required: A portfolio of research endeavours and outcomes that emphasizes publications in high-quality, peer-reviewed journals.  Teaching experience in undergraduate business analytics or supply chain courses. Preferred: Professional experience in a related field.  Demonstrated record of implementing experiential, case based, collaborative, or other high impact teaching methods. Demonstrated effectiveness in teaching undergraduate business analytics or supply chain courses. Demonstrated commitment to Trinity’s value of intentional inclusion. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants , you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  Each as a separate pdf file [note: a maximum of five (5) files can be uploaded]: Cover Lette r – addressing how your skills/abilities align with Trinity University’s mission and with the position requirements. Generic cover letters will invalidate the application. Curriculum Vitae (CV) with names and email addresses of three references (references may be contacted to submit reference letters electronically).  Documentation of Teaching Effectiveness – including representative syllabi, list of courses taught, examples of innovative teaching practices, and official original, complete course evaluations with student comments (Items need to be combined in one file). One or More Research Publications – in one or more of the desired research areas mentioned in job duties and responsibilities (Publications need to be combined into one file). Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. CONTACT INFORMATION Dr. Suning Zhu ( szhu1@trinity.edu ), Search Committee Chair Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Senior-Director-of-Advancement-Resources_JR101611": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Advancement Resources",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-24\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Alumni Relations and Development Time Type: Full time Compensation: $100,304.80-$125,381.01 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Provides strategic oversight of the budget, design, development, and implementation of an industry-leading advancement resources (operations & research) model that integrates research, report writing, data analytics, technology solutions, and gift processing to maximize engagement and philanthropy of Trinity University alumni, parents, students, and friends. Serves as a member of the University’s Alumni Relations and Development Senior Leadership Team, while leading a team. Responsible for strategic design, development, and implementation of operational functions that provide a foundation for University Advancement to achieve its goals in fundraising and engagement. Leads and manages teams related to advancement reporting, research/prospect management, gift accounting and data management. Aligns business support functions, policies and procedures, and technology infrastructure with the strategies, resources and priorities of the University and the Alumni Relations and Development division. Represents the division across campus by working closely with business units such as finance, human resources/payroll, student accounts, registrars, information technology, general counsel, and risk management/compliance. Please note: applications submitted without a cover letter will not be reviewed.   JOB DUTIES Develops strategy, sets direction, and defines goals and priorities for advancement resources while serving as a member of the senior leadership team. Provides benchmarking data to senior directors and the division’s leadership from peer and aspirant universities. Investigates, assesses, selects and manages technology solutions that will advance the fundraising and engagement goals of the division as well as optimize the division’s operations. Defines roles, responsibilities, and expectations for advancement resources (operations & research) staff and manages them accordingly. Oversees a budget to support Advancement Resources. Oversees policies and protocols that achieve success in principal gifts, major gifts and planned giving, advancement communications, donor relations & special events, annual giving, and alumni relations departments and sub-teams..  Evaluates current business practices and resources to optimize performance for fundraising and engagement success. Includes oversight of training efforts to ensure ARD staff productively utilizes software/systems and processes which supports and facilitates increased fundraising. Oversees the building and implementation of best-in-class prospect management, gift processing, advancement reporting (ad hoc and scheduled) and data/database management ( Affinaquest ), artificial intelligence, utilization of innovative toolsets (dashboards, modeling, new products/services and analytics studies, etc.) while encouraging data-driven management in fundraising and boosting the University’s fundraising success at divisional, departmental, and team levels. Also, oversees the timely delivery of information, data, and services to staff members, volunteers and constituents with accurate reporting and analysis tools, maintaining both accuracy of information and consistency of presentation. Produces overall vision and strategy, as well as management and operations for gift accounting for the University to ensure accurate and timely recording and distribution of gifts and pledges according to IRS regulations, CASE reporting standards, and associated University’s gift policies and procedures i.e. gift acceptance and naming, etc. Integrates and oversees a comprehensive prospect development team, including prospect identification and research, as well as prospect and portfolio management. Delivers innovative technology, as well as ground-breaking data systems and processes that support a complex, highly matrixed fundraising and engagement environment. Researches new technology solutions, tests with internal partners and supports delivery with on campus partners. Oversee all departmental functions and procedures including data integrity, processing, transfer, training, queries, reporting and special projects. Develops partnerships and collaborates with key business units across campus including the University’s ITS and Business Office areas to solve current problems, build frameworks for operational efficiencies in fundraising, and develop processes/procedures which are donor friendly and better supports and facilitates increased fundraising. Directs, coordinates, and evaluates Advancement Resources team members.  Carries out supervisory responsibilities in accordance with all policies and applicable laws.  Interviews, hires and trains employees; plans, assigns, and directs work; creates work schedules and conducts performance appraisals, counsels, and disciplines employees; addresses complaints and resolves problems. ADDITIONAL DUTIES  Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience and integrity to provide professional leadership during emergencies. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Performs other duties as required. EDUCATION Required: Bachelor's degree from four-year college or university. Preferred: Master's degree or equivalent from an accredited college or university in data science, business, or other similar advanced degree. EXPERIENCE Required: Seven+ years of progressively responsible experience in data analysis and reporting, operations, information technology, and other areas of administrative and operational management in higher education or the equivalent, preferably in an advancement program recognized for its strength and effectiveness. Seven years of management/supervisory experience. Experience working with fundraising software systems (such as Affinaquest , GiveCampus, Stripe,or other platforms) and related reporting tools (Informer, Business Objects, etc.). Preferred: Advanced knowledge of development activities with particular emphasis on information systems, development services, prospect research, fundraising and alumni data programs and procedures. Demonstrated talent in developing and implementing strategies, plans, and budgets in a complex, fast changing environment that utilize clearly articulated, metric-based, analytical solutions to complex problems. Proven ability to communicate effectively both verbally and in writing, and to collaborate and work closely with senior level administrators, advancement staff, and alumni volunteers with confidence and diplomacy. Expert skills in employee supervision and personnel administration; strong interpersonal skills and high emotional intelligence to effectively lead, motivate, and influence others, as well as recruit, hire, train, mentor, motivate, and evaluate all levels of staff. Excellent organizational skills and attention to detail including experience independently overseeing complex projects and programs. The ability to build bridges and solid, productive, collaborative relationships across a complex university, as well as the capability to influence and engage peers. Effectiveness as a change agent, with the ability to think strategically, to turn new ideas and approaches into executable programs, and lead a diverse staff through ongoing changes in organization, processes, procedures, and technologies. A resilient and self-assured problem solver who can anticipate challenges and obstacles and has the intelligence, creativity, and tenacity to provide and execute strategic and effective solutions. Proficiency in managing multiple projects simultaneously with diverse and competing priorities, while meeting deadlines. Ability to handle highly confidential information with respect and discretion. Proficiency in interpreting IRS rulings as they apply to charitable contributions and fundraising. Unflappable in the face of multiple challenges, highly motivated, disciplined work ethic, a competitive nature, a creative approach, and a proven ability to lead. Demonstrated talent in translating ideas into action; a calculated risk-taker comfortable working with ambiguity. Integrity, imagination, optimism, entrepreneurial spirit, and a high-level of energy. Personal flexibility to travel and work evenings or weekends as required. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Required: Working knowledge of advancement best practices and strong understanding of data management. Working knowledge of Microsoft software suites, including Word, Excel, and Access. Working knowledge of Google Workspace, including Google calendar, Gmail, Google shared drives, Google docs, and Google sheets. Working knowledge with relational databases, such as Salesforce solutions such as Affineurs or other fundraising database software, as well as integrated reporting tools such as Apsona , Tableau, MS Power BI; and connected third party software solutions, such as I-Modules, RNL-Engage/Scale funder, EverTrue, DocuSign, etc. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS None OTHER REQUIREMENTS Travel will be uncommon, but some travel may be necessary. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES Director, through managers, of a single department. NUMBER OF DIRECT REPORTS 4 NUMBER OF INDIRECT REPORTS 5-7 SUPERVISION RECEIVED Limited supervision.  Operates independently. DECISION MAKING Plan and perform highly complex or technical work where no procedures or standard methods are available. BUDGET RESPONSIBILITY Responsible for budget and expenditure authority of the department. FINANCIAL RESPONSIBILITY Less than $250,000 HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, please note that applications that do not include a cover letter will not be reviewed. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  Submit each as a separate pdf file: File 1: Cover Letter File 2: Resume"
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Assistant-Professor_JR101616": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Modern Languages and Literatures - French",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-22\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Modern Languages & Literatures Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Modern Languages and Literatures at Trinity University (San Antonio, Texas) invites applications for the tenure-track position of Assistant Professor of French Studies. The successful applicant will hold a Ph.D. in French or closely related fields, such as Francophone Studies, Comparative Literature, Transnational or Diaspora Studies, and demonstrate a clear expertise in French speaking Northern/Sub-Saharan African, or Caribbean, literatures and cultures. The successful applicant will also showcase the ability and desire to teach French at all levels, including French for professional purposes (such as French for business or French for diplomacy), and to take part in Trinity’s newly established summer abroad program in Aix-en-Provence, as an example of service to the Department and the University. Lastly, the ideal candidate will welcome the possibility of research and teaching collaboration(s) across the University, and be open to teach interdisciplinary, cross listed courses in English on an as-needed basis. The regular teaching load for this position is five courses per year (2/3 or 3/2), with a course reduction during the first year, all at the undergraduate level.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . See the following links for information about  Trinity’s curriculum , including the  First-Year Experience ,  experiential learning , and faculty-led  study-abroad programs . The Carlos Alvarez School of Arts and Humanities, endowed with a gift of $25 million , is at the center of Trinity's liberal arts education. Faculty are international leaders in their respective fields, creating engaged learning experiences grounded in careful study of tradition, critical thinking, clear communication, and creative expression. The Department of Modern Languages and Literatures (ML&L) is the largest and most ethnically diverse academic unit at Trinity University. It offers majors in five languages (Chinese, French, German, Russian, Spanish), in Global Latinx Studies, a minor in Comparative Literature as well as beginning and intermediate Italian and Japanese language courses. ML&L takes pride in offering various opportunities for our students to study abroad, conduct research, and partake in experiential learning experiences. We work closely with the Mexico, the Americas, and Spain (MAS) , the East Asian Studies at Trinity (EAST) programs, and the City of San Antonio. Both the Department of Modern Languages and Literatures  and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Demonstrate a strong commitment to language teaching and pedagogy, as well as the ability to offer rigorous and creative courses for the French Studies curriculum at the beginner, intermediate, and advanced levels, including French for professional purposes (business, diplomacy, teaching). Present an active research agenda with a clear focus on Francophone literature and/or cultures, e.g. Northern/Sub-Saharan Africa, the Caribbean, etc. Note that interdisciplinary research is strongly encouraged.  Support the French Studies program, the Department of Modern Languages and Literatures, and the Carlos Alvarez School of Arts and Humanities by collaborating closely with its faculty, which includes participating in our summer abroad program in Aix-en-Provence. Be able to develop interdisciplinary courses in English to teach cross listed courses in such areas as Women’s and Gender Studies, Black Studies, Comparative Literature, International Studies, Environmental Humanities, and Trinity’s First-Year Experience, in order to promote Trinity’s Pathways curriculum.  Be fluent (native or near native) in both French and English; we welcome all varieties of French and English spoken around the world. APPOINTMENT DETAILS This position will be a 9-month appointment (9 or 12 month disbursement) starting in August 2027. QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Ph.D in French or Francophone Studies (or closely related areas) by the start of the appointment EXPERIENCE Required: Teaching experience in French at the undergraduate level. Ongoing research agenda in relevant fields of Francophone literature and cultures. Preferred: Teaching experience in French at all undergraduate levels (beginner, intermediate and/or advanced), and experience teaching French for professional purposes. Study abroad experience. KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES Native or near native fluency in both French and English ATTENDANCE This position is not remote. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants , you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  Each as a separate pdf file [note: a maximum of five (5) files can be uploaded]: File 1: Letter of application. File 2: Curriculum Vitae, with contact information for three letters of reference File 3: Teaching evaluations (all levels). ] File 4: Writing sample (article or chapter-length) in French or English from the applicant’s dissertation or peer-reviewed publication (scientific journal or edited volume). File 5: Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. A maximum of three references may be requested from candidates at a later stage of the search process.   The application deadline is October 16, 2026.  Late applications will not be considered. CONTACT INFORMATION Please email any questions you may have to Dr. Maxence Leconte, Chair of the search committee: mllsearch@trinity.edu . Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Political-Science_JR101595": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Political Science",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-17\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Political Science Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Political Science invites applications for a tenure track position in Comparative Politics at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning August 2027. The successful candidate should have the PhD completed by August 2027. The department is open to various area and regional specializations, but is particularly interested in thematic fields such as (but not limited to): environmental politics, migration, refugees, global health, democratic instability, media, social movements, and political economy and inequality. Trinity University values both strong teaching and research. The successful candidate is expected to maintain a robust research agenda, supported by competitive summer research stipends and pre-tenure academic leave. The successful candidate will teach the lower division survey course in Comparative Politics and upper division courses in their areas of expertise. The candidate will also be expected to contribute to the university’s general education curriculum, particularly its First-Year Experience. A strong commitment to undergraduate teaching is essential, as well as a demonstrated commitment to Trinity’s value of intentional inclusion. There are many opportunities for Trinity faculty to be engaged in experiential learning, interdisciplinary teaching and research, and faculty-led study abroad programs. The teaching load consists of five courses per academic year.   Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . The Department of Political Science is part of the School of Social Science & Civic Engagement, home to six academic departments and four interdisciplinary programs. The School has a long record of involvement in interdisciplinary research and teaching, both within the School and across the University. It prizes experiential learning opportunities for Trinity students, including research with faculty, consulting for local civic organizations, and teaching in San Antonio schools. Trinity University’s political science department is composed of nine full-time faculty members, teaching a range of courses across the core subfields of American Politics, Comparative Politics, International Relations, and Political Theory, as well as Public Law and Research Methods. The department supports interdisciplinary programs such as International Studies, Urban Studies, East Asian Studies, Environmental Studies, Mexican American Studies, Medieval and Renaissance Studies, German Studies, as well as Women’s, Gender, and Sexuality Studies. Both the Department of Political Science and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching Teaching responsibilities will include both lower and upper-level courses in the candidate’s area of interest and expertise that complement the department curriculum. Participation in the First-Year Experience program is expected. The teaching load is 3-2 courses per year.    Research   Consistent and productive scholarly activity is expected, including attendance at professional meetings, participation in professional organizations, presentations of papers at academic conferences, and publication of peer-reviewed research.     Service Faculty are expected to advise and mentor our majors, and engage in service to the department, the university, the profession, and the community.  QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: A PhD in Political Science is required, to be completed by August 2027.  Preferred: A primary field of doctoral specialization in Comparative Politics EXPERIENCE Preferred: A demonstrated commitment to undergraduate education is preferred. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants , you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below, each as a separate pdf file [note: a maximum of five (5) files can be uploaded]: File 1: Cover letter, Curriculum Vitae, and Transcripts File 2:Three letters of recommendation File 3: Evidence of teaching effectiveness (such as a teaching philosophy and course evaluations) File 4: Writing sample File 5: Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. Deadline to submit applications is September 30, 2026. CONTACT INFORMATION David Crockett Search Committee Chair Department of Political Science Phone: (210) 999-8342 Email: dcrocket@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/AmeriCorps-College-Advisor_JR101518": {
      "title": "AmeriCorps College Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-13\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: AmeriCorps College Advising Time Type: Full time Compensation: $35,360.00-$44,035.88 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Trinity University’s College Advising Corps program is accepting applications for the 2026-2027 Academic Year. This position is contingent upon continuous funding. Position: College Adviser for Trinity University’s College Advising Corps –multiple openings. The position will remain open until all positions are filled. This position flat rate is dependent on start date with the program. The person selected for this role MUST reside in Texas and have transportation to school site. The College Advisor serve local high schools in San Antonio. Working alongside high school and district staff, the adviser will assist students with college admissions, financial aid, and scholarship applications to help student realize their dream of attending college. Reports to: Sarah Ramos, Project Director, in conjunction with an on-site liaison at the assigned high school above.   The College Advisor will serve at local partnership high school in San Antonio or the surrounding area. Working alongside high school and district staff, the adviser will assist students with college admissions, financial aid, and scholarship applications to help student realize their dream of attending college. Qualifications and Characteristics Unquestioned personal integrity, credibility, maturity, and work ethic Energy, enthusiasm and self-confidence in building relationships with students, public school partners, the university and the state and national organizations Flexibility and adaptability in work styles and structures Ability to collaborate creatively and effectively with a variety of people to solve problems and reach goals Passion for making college access available to all students and for serving the community Excellent organizational and communication skills, including the ability to listen carefully and to write and speak persuasively Responsibilities Provide one-on-one admissions and financial aid advice to any student or family seeking assistance Organize group events that encourage students and their families to consider, plan for, and apply to colleges and universities Establish productive working relationships with principals, counselors, and teachers in the assigned high school Assist in the efforts of other advisers within the service area Assist in the assessment and long-term sustainability of the program Maintain expertise in admissions and financial-aid advising (extensive training will be provided) Weekend and evening commitments will be expected Requirements Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution required. Experience in working in a high school environment in similar role recommended Demonstration of excellent written and verbal communication as well as strong interpersonal skills Proficiency in the use of computers for communication, research, and administrative recordkeeping Advisors will be expected to live within the service area and to involve themselves in the surrounding San Antonio community Advisors will need to provide their own transportation to the school site as well as to related events Experience working with high school students preferred Bilingual (Spanish / English) candidates preferred but not required Trinity graduates who are the first in their family to graduate from college are highly encouraged to apply Compensation (contingent upon grant funding) This a paid AmeriCorps position that provides the candidate with a monthly $2,745 living stipend, AmeriCorps healthcare, and sick/vacation leave during their term. The is an 11-month appointment with an August 10, 2026 start date (subject to change). Upon successful completion of the appointment of service and 1,700 required hours of service, the advisor may be eligible for an Education Award of $7,395 to use towards federal undergraduate student loans and/or a graduate education. Candidates may also receive an extension for a 2nd year of service upon successful completion of service. The position is contingent upon continuous grant funding. Application Deadline: Applications for initial search will be accepted through August 28, 2026. The position will remain open until all positions are filled. Interested candidates may apply by submitting the following: A Letter of Interest that outlines your desire and qualifications to work with a college access program in a public high school AND addresses the following questions: Why are you interested in serving as an Adviser with the National College Advising Corps program at Trinity University? What will make you an effective adviser? How do you believe this experience will benefit you both personally and professionally? Resume Two (2) Letters of Recommendation that speak to your qualifications for this position."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Part-Time-Faculty---Philosophy_JR101654": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - Philosophy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nPart time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Part time Department/Office: Philosophy Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Trinity University is seeking a Part-Time Faculty for the Spring 2027 semester to teach the following courses: PHIL-3431-1: Philosophy of Mind, AND EITHER: PHIL-1301-1: Knowledge, Reality, and the Good Life OR: PHIL 1354-1: Ethics   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Typical responsibilities of part-time faculty members include but are not limited to: Developing the course syllabus and ensuring that the syllabus meets departmental and University standards Designing and delivering lectures, discussions, in-class activities, and assignments Grading assigned papers, quizzes, and exams Assigning early, midterm, and final grades in compliance with University deadlines Maintaining office hours as directed by the department chair or program director Making student accommodations as determined by the Office of Student Accessibility Services Adhering to all instructional policies as published in the Faculty Handbook and the Courses of Study Bulletin   APPOINTMENT DETAILS This position is a non-renewable, one-semester appointment. QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Ph.D. or ABD in Philosophy  EXPERIENCE Preferred: Experience teaching philosophy of mind to undergraduates KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES The ideal candidate will demonstrate: Mastery of the philosophy of mind Ability to deliver engaging lectures, discussions, and in-class activities Ability to design rigorous and meaningful assignments Commitment to the liberal arts mission of Trinity University   HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants, please apply through Workday using the 'Jobs Hub' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants, when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following, each as a separate pdf file [note: a maximum of five (5) files can be uploaded]: Cover letter  Curriculum vitae or resume  Names and contact information for three professional references CONTACT INFORMATION Dr. Andrew Kania, Search Committee Chair, Department of Philosophy, akania@trinity.edu  Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Executive-Coordinator-to-the-VP-Administrative-Manager_JR101659": {
      "title": "Executive Coordinator to the VP Administrative Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Vice President for Alumni Relations and Development Time Type: Full time Compensation: $47,026.82-$58,783.52 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Responsible for supervising the Administrative Specialists team that supports the departments and offices of the Alumni Relations and Development (AR&D) Division. Supports the goals and mission objectives of the AR&D Division and Trinity University. Coordinates calendars, communications, reports, and logistics for the VP Office, while maintaining a professional presence and demeanor supporting the Division’s various internal and external stakeholders. Supports the VP Office’s management of the Advancement Committee of the Board of Trustees. Helps to facilitate positive and collaborative relationships. Serves as an active supportive member to the Senior Staff and will be present for Senior Staff meetings and retreats.   JOB DUTIES Directs, coordinates, and evaluates Administrative Specialists.  Carries out supervisory responsibilities in accordance with all policies and applicable laws.  Interviews, hires, and trains Administrative Specialists; conducts performance appraisals, counsels, and disciplines Administrative Specialists; addresses complaints and resolves problems. Also supervises student workers. Processes VP and AVP expense reports on monthly basis and works closely with the Business Office/P-Card Office to ensure the divisional expense reports are completed in a timely manner. Works with the AVP to manage the annual operating budget for the VP Office. Coordinates communication of projects, routine and special events, and information from the VP Office i.e., conferences, workshops, faculty and staff retreats, staff meetings, office morale events, banquets, and other routine or special events as they arise. Manages the daily calendar for the VP and AVP.  Schedules meetings, events, and other activities, as requested. Initiates facilities requests for 323 Stadium Drive and oversees office management for the building. Maintains accurate and up-to-date files on official policies relating to the Alumni Relations and Development Division i.e., gift acceptance policy, naming and honoring policy, etc.. Maintains updated information on various university related committees and committee members from the AR&D Division.  Manages the VP Office’s travel arrangements. Supports various groups across all levels, including VP, AVPs, and Division Senior Staff members. Supports Advancement Resources with administrative tasks as needed. Answers telephones professionally, greets visitors, makes appointments, and provides information on behalf of the VP, AVPs, and division, as necessary. ADDITIONAL DUTIES Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience and integrity to provide professional leadership during emergencies. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Performs other duties as required. EDUCATION Required: Bachelor's degree from a four-year college or university or equivalent experience. EXPERIENCE Required: Three years of progressively responsible administrative/executive level support experience or seven years of progressively responsible administrative/executive level support experience in lieu of education. Experience as an Office Manager, Administrative Manager, Executive Assistant, or a comparable position. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Required: Excellent organizational and time management skills. Ability to meet deadlines. Thorough knowledge of Google Workspace. Demonstrated proficiency in MS Office with advanced knowledge of Word, Excel, and PowerPoint. Demonstrated ability to independently perform the wide range of supervisory, administrative, and technical responsibilities associated with this position. Ability to learn new software quickly. Use and knowledge of word processing programs, online and cloud-based file management systems, and other standard office machines. Independent judgment, specialized knowledge, coordination of office functions, and activities of direct reports. Ability to learn quickly, particularly with internal systems. Effective interpersonal skills. Ability to work effectively in a cross-departmental environment. Effective verbal and written communication skills. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS Required: Must possess a valid Texas Driver’s License and have a good driving history.  May not have more than two moving violations in a three-year period. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES Supervisor over a section of a department or group of employees. NUMBER OF DIRECT REPORTS 3 (not including students) NUMBER OF INDIRECT REPORTS None DECISION MAKING Work where procedures are of varied or diversified nature. BUDGET RESPONSIBILITY Monitors budget activities but no responsibility for budget and expenditure authority. FINANCIAL RESPONSIBILITY Less than $250,000"
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Part-Time-Faculty---General-Education_JR101658": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - General Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Part time Department/Office: Philosophy Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Trinity University is seeking a Part-Time Faculty for the Spring 2027 semester to teach the following course: GNED-3430-1: Moral Imagination and US Incarceration   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Typical responsibilities of part-time faculty members include but are not limited to: Developing the course syllabus and ensuring that the syllabus meets departmental and University standards Designing and delivering lectures, discussions, in-class activities, and assignments Grading assigned papers, quizzes, and exams Assigning early, midterm, and final grades in compliance with University deadlines Maintaining office hours as directed by the department chair or program director Making student accommodations as determined by the Office of Student Accessibility Services Adhering to all instructional policies as published in the Faculty Handbook and the Courses of Study Bulletin   APPOINTMENT DETAILS This position is a non-renewable, one-semester appointment. The instructor will be responsible for coordinating the experiential component of the class, a weekly learning circle inside a prison. The instructor will need to arrange for the transportation of students to the prison and the coordination and oversight of students while at the prison.   QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: A master’s degree in a relevant subject. EXPERIENCE Required: Prior experience in facilitating the Philosophy and Literature Circle with undergraduate students at a Texas prison or jail. KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES The ideal candidate will demonstrate: Mastery of the relevant subject matter Ability to deliver engaging lectures, discussions, or in-class activities Ability to design rigorous and meaningful assignments Commitment to the liberal arts mission of Trinity University PHYSICAL DEMANDS AND WORKING CONDITIONS Successful candidate must be able to drive a van to the program site.    HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants, please apply through Workday using the 'Jobs Hub' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants, when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following, each as a separate pdf file [note: a maximum of five (5) files can be uploaded]: Cover letter  Curriculum vitae or resume  Names and contact information for three professional references   CONTACT INFORMATION Dr. Andrew Kania, Search Committee Chair, Department of Philosophy, akania@trinity.edu  Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Adjunct-Part-Time-Professor--Political-Science-Urban-Studies_JR101655": {
      "title": "Adjunct Part-Time Professor, Political Science/Urban Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Part time Department/Office: Sociology & Anthropology Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Trinity University is seeking a Part-Time Faculty for the Spring 2027 semester to teach the following course: PLSI 34/URBS 3416: Urban Politics An exploration of the urban space, including the politics, institutions, power centers, policies, and the people who live in urban environments. Emphasis is given to why cities matter, how cities are organized, how cities operate as an economic engine, and how cities shape identity and experiences while producing inequality. Contemporary urban issues are considered, such as demographic changes, community power, and public transportation.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Typical responsibilities of part-time faculty members include but are not limited to: Developing the course syllabus and ensuring that the syllabus meets departmental and University standards Designing and delivering lectures, discussions, in-class activities, and assignments Grading assigned papers, quizzes, and exams Assigning early, midterm, and final grades in compliance with University deadlines Maintaining office hours as directed by the department chair or program director Making student accommodations as determined by the Office of Student Accessibility Services Adhering to all instructional policies as published in the Faculty Handbook and the Courses of Study Bulletin   EDUCATION Required: Masters degree or equivalent in political science, urban studies, or a related field. EXPERIENCE Preferred:   University teaching/classroom experience preferred. KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES The ideal candidate will demonstrate: Mastery of the relevant subject matter Ability to deliver engaging lectures, discussions, or in-class activities Ability to design rigorous and meaningful assignments Commitment to the liberal arts mission of Trinity University HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants, please apply through Workday using the 'Jobs Hub' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants, when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following, each as a separate pdf file [note: a maximum of five (5) files can be uploaded]: File 1: Cover letter  File 2: Curriculum vitae or resume  File 3: Names and contact information for three professional references File 4: For applicants who have taught an upper-division undergraduate university course in Urban Politics, please provide a copy of the syllabus used. Other applicants should provide a summary of the topics and approaches they would anticipate using in such a course. CONTACT INFORMATION Christine Drennon, Search Committee Chair, Urban Studies Program, cdrennon@trinity.edu   Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Assistant-Professor-of-Art---Drawing-and-Painting_JR101652": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Art - Drawing and Painting",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Art & Art History Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Art and Art History at Trinity University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position in Studio Art at the rank of assistant professor focusing on painting and drawing, to begin in August 2027. We seek applicants with expertise in painting and drawing grounded in studio practice, scholarship, and teaching. Candidates should have an active exhibition record and demonstrated fluency in painting and drawing. Artist-educators who bring innovative approaches and an interdisciplinary perspective to their studio practice and teaching, and whose work engages with the expanded field of painting, are encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will have the ability to teach strong foundational skills and to engage students in contemporary issues and conceptual dimensions of artmaking within a liberal arts context. The successful candidate will maintain a vigorous studio practice and a strong exhibition record, while demonstrating a firm commitment to teaching at the undergraduate level. The candidate will be expected to teach introductory lower-division studio courses as well as advanced classes and seminar courses for art majors. All faculty at Trinity are expected to contribute to Pathways, the University’s interdisciplinary general education program.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . San Antonio supports a vibrant contemporary art scene and an array of museums, galleries, and art venues, including Artpace, The Contemporary at Blue Star, Ruby City, the McNay Art Museum and San Antonio Museum of Art. The Carlos Alvarez School of Arts and Humanities, endowed with a gift of $25 million , is at the center of Trinity's liberal arts education. Faculty are international leaders in their respective fields, creating engaged learning experiences grounded in careful study of tradition, critical thinking, clear communication, and creative expression. Comprising five studio artists and five art historians, the Department of Art and Art History offers majors and minors in Studio Art, Art History and a minor in Architectural Studies. The Department is housed in the Dicke Art Building, which features fully equipped state-of-the-art studio facilities as well as the Michael & Noémi Neidorff Art Gallery, a non-collecting gallery that features faculty- and student-led exhibitions. The Department maintains strong connections with several other departments and interdisciplinary programs on campus, including Education, Environmental Studies, Human Communication and Theatre, Media and Communication, Modern Languages and Literatures, Museum Studies, and Urban Studies. We encourage our students to pursue research, often in collaboration with faculty, and actively participate in the university’s undergraduate research programs. Both the Department of Art and Art History and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching:  Teach lower and upper-level courses in painting and drawing and the art major seminar courses, under a (typically) 3-2 teaching load. Contribute to the development, planning, and implementation of a high-quality curriculum in introductory through advanced courses and develop new courses based on area of specialization Contribute to the maintenance and initiatives for improvement of the drawing and painting studios and workspaces   Research:   Maintain a consistent and productive artistic practice, to include participation in exhibitions and related professional activities.   Service: Provide appropriate service to the department, the university, and the profession, to include advising and contribution to the development, planning and implementation of departmental event programming. QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: MFA in Studio Art (in painting or a related discipline)  EXPERIENCE Required: One-year college-level teaching experience (teaching assistantship may fulfill this requirement)  Demonstrated record of professional achievement commensurate with years of training, experience, and studio practice. Preferred: The ideal candidate will have two or more years of undergraduate teaching experience  KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES ​ Expertise in area of specialization and working knowledge of a range of traditional and contemporary materials and approaches. Knowledge of art historical and contemporary issues related to the expanded field of drawing and painting practices. Knowledge of material and equipment safety in studios.   PHYSICAL DEMANDS AND WORKING CONDITIONS ​ Work in drawing and painting studio settings around wet and dry art materials, including acrylic and oil paint, mediums, and solvents. Maintain studio environment with assistance from studio manager and student assistants. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants , please apply through Workday using the ' Jobs Hub ' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants , when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants , you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  File 1: Cover letter addressing teaching experience and philosophy, and creative research interests (3 pages max)  File 2: Curriculum vitae, including the names and contact information for three professional references (they will only be contacted if you are selected as finalist)  File 3: Artist statement and up to 20 images of examples of artwork; individual images should be labeled with title, date, materials, and dimensions. File 4: Syllabus and student work images (up to 15, labeled with materials and dimensions) File 5: Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. CONTACT INFORMATION Jessica Halonen, Professor of Art Search Committee Chair, Department of Art and Art History Email: art_arthistory_jobsearch@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Part-Time-Assistant-Athletic-Trainer_JR101643": {
      "title": "Part Time Assistant Athletic Trainer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Athletic Trainers Time Type: Part time Compensation: Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Responsible for the coordination and administration of all aspects of the athletic training room and sports medicine offerings for the intercollegiate athletic programs as deemed by the Head Athletic Trainer.   JOB DUTIES Onsite care and management of injuries and illnesses including: prevention, evaluation, management and rehabilitation. Provides accommodation recommendations, related to sport, for student athletes with restrictions or limitations. Assists with accident insurance claims processing.  Minimizes risk of injury through awareness, education and prevention strategies.  Maintains student athlete insurance information. Assists with training, scheduling and payroll for student staff. Assists with physical examinations and screenings. Assists with drug testing per department policy.  Makes appropriate physician’s referrals and appointments. Communicates with physicians and coaching staff regarding the injury status of athletes. Assists the Athletic Director and coaching staff in determining whether the athletic facility is in optimal and safe condition.  Helps monitor weather conditions and dangerous environments (humidity/lightning). Coordinates the purchasing and use of all equipment and supplies needed for the operation of the athletic training facility, in accordance with University policies. Adheres to conference guidelines, including visiting team accommodations and services. ADDITIONAL DUTIES  Abides by NCAA Division III rules and regulations. Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience and integrity to provide professional leadership during emergencies. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Performs other duties as required.   EDUCATION  Required: Bachelor's degree from four-year college or university.   Preferred: Master's degree or equivalent from four-year college or university.   EXPERIENCE  Required: One year of experience with an intercollegiate athletic program. Administrative experience with operating an athletic training room.   Preferred: Three years of experience."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Groundskeeper_JR101665": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Groundskeeping Time Type: Full time Compensation: Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Non-Exempt Job Description: Cleans and maintains University properties. Performs relatively simple, semi-skilled duties which include litter picking, trash and debris removal, sweeping sidewalks and courtyards, leaf removal, mulching, mowing, other general landscaping maintenance, uses hand and power equipment and tools.   JOB DUTIES Mows, trims, waters and provides general campus grounds maintenance, as needed. Operates power equipment such as, but not limited to, mowers, edgers, hedge trimmers, and leaf blowers. Picks up campus litter and empties trash receptacles. Occasionally performs general maintenance such as landscape installations, basic constructions, and power washing of university property, etc. Cleans parking garages and parking lots. Performs ice clean up, cleans storm sewers, cleans damage from hail, rain, and high wind. May occasionally assists other landscape services or facilities services departments with tasks of a routine, unspecialized nature (warehouse cleaning, stocking, furniture moving, events set-up, etc.). Mulches, plants and installs plant materials such as trees, shrubs, grass and ground cover. ADDITIONAL DUTIES Cooperates and collaborates with other employees in the spirit of teamwork and collegiality and interacts with confidence, patience and integrity to provide professional leadership during emergencies. Complies with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Operates, cleans and performs preventive maintenance such as greasing, checking and filling fluids, etc. on all equipment and vehicles. Performs other duties as required. EDUCATION Required: None Preferred: High School Diploma or GED. EXPERIENCE Required: None. Preferred: One year of general lawn care experience. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Required: Ability to read, write and follow written and verbal instructions Exhibits necessary communication skills, verbal and written Portrays excellent attention to detail and exemplary customer service skills and/or willingness to assist and/or work with others Demonstrated regular, reliable, and predictable attendance Must be able to interrelate with fellow employees as needed to get the job done Must treat fellow employees, students, staff and visitors to the University with respect Attention to accuracy in all work. Preferred: Bilingual (Spanish and English). LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS Required: Must possess a valid Texas Driver’s License and have a good driving history.  May not have more than two moving violations in a three-year period. OTHER REQUIREMENTS All jobs require a current Criminal Background Check (CBC). Must be able to pass a physical examination."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Visiting-Assistant-Professor---Business-Analytics_JR101672": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor - Business Analytics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Finance and Business Analytics Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Finance and Business Analytics in the Neidorff School of Business at Trinity University invites applications for a Visiting Assistant Professor position in Business Analytics, beginning January 2027 and ending in May 2027. Applicants must hold a PhD or be ABD in Business Analytics or Applied Statistics. The successful candidate will teach three sections of BAT2301 - Statistics for Business as well as one section of BAT3302 - Data Science during the Spring 2027 semester. In addition to these teaching duties, the candidate will be responsible for attending school/departmental meetings, holding regular office hours to support students, and completing other miscellaneous tasks as directed by the department chair.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . At the Neidorff School of Business at Trinity University, innovation meets impact. Located in the 7th largest city in the United States, where we’re redefining what it means to deliver a modern business education with bold ideas, ethical leadership, and global perspective coming together. Our AACSB-accredited programs - ranked #1 among undergraduate business schools in Texas and #27 in the nation - empower students to think strategically, lead confidently, and make ethical decisions grounded in data and purpose.   Our faculty are mentors, teacher-scholars, and catalysts for transformation. The Neidorff School thrives on collaboration across disciplines, forging powerful connections with humanities, social sciences and STEM disciplines. With cutting-edge facilities, strong industry partnerships, and a student-centered culture that prizes creativity and inclusion, Neidorff is a dynamic hub for shaping the next generation of business leaders who make a difference in the world.  Join us, and be part of a school that’s driven by purpose, powered by people, and poised for the future. The Department of Finance and Business Analytics offers two undergraduate majors—Finance and Business Analytics—and is home to 13 dedicated faculty committed to teaching excellence and student success. The Business Analytics group, a close-knit team of 7 faculty supporting about 50 majors, delivers a rigorous and applied curriculum grounded in the liberal arts tradition. Students gain hands-on experience through industry sponsored projects and national analytics competitions that connect classroom learning to real-world business analytics. The department fosters a collaborative, student-centered environment that emphasizes teaching innovation, student career readiness, and the integration of finance and analytics. Both the Department of Finance and Business Analytics and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teach 3 face-to-face sections of Statistics for Business during the Spring 2027 semester Teach 1 face-to-face section of Data Science during the Spring 2027 semester Hold regular office hours to support students Participate in school/department meetings during the Spring 2027 semester Complete miscellaneous tasks as requested by the department chair QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Complete a PhD in either Business Analytics or Statistics or achieved ABD status in one of those fields . EXPERIENCE Required: At least 2 semesters of undergraduate teaching experience in Business Statistics or Data Science HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants, please apply through Workday using the 'Jobs Hub' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants, when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  Cover Letter – addressing how your skills/abilities align with Trinity University’s mission and with the position requirements. Curriculum Vitae (CV) Documentation of Teaching Effectiveness Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. CONTACT INFORMATION Dr. Diana K. Young; dyoung1@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Lecturer---Lighting-Designer_JR101661": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Lighting Designer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Human Communication and Theatre Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: The Department of Human Communication and Theatre at Trinity University invites applications for a Lecturer/Lighting Designer position, beginning August 2027. The department seeks a collegial artist with the ability to collaborate with a production team, the desire to design in a variety of theatrical styles, and a commitment to instruction and production in an undergraduate liberal arts and sciences context. We are seeking a candidate with a commitment to excellence while working collegially with faculty colleagues and supportively with students. The candidate will have expertise as a lighting designer. Preference will be given to those candidates with secondary working knowledge in sound design and/or projection. The successful candidate will teach four courses per academic year in design and/or other relevant areas of theatre in our BA program. In addition, the successful candidate will design lighting for all Theatre productions (typically four per academic year). The candidate will also be responsible for the implementation and execution of designs, while training and supporting student workers. We are especially interested in candidates with experience mentoring undergraduate student designers.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . The Carlos Alvarez School of Arts and Humanities, endowed with a gift of $25 million , is at the center of Trinity's liberal arts education. Faculty are international leaders in their respective fields, creating engaged learning experiences grounded in careful study of tradition, critical thinking, clear communication, and creative expression. Both the Department of Theatre and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teach four courses per academic year in Design and/or other areas of theatre. Design Lighting (and Sound/Projection as applicable) for all Trinity Theatre productions, which rotate across classical, contemporary, and global forms.  Responsible for the execution and implementation of designs. Train and Supervise student workers during daily shop hours Mon-Fri 2:30-5:30. Advise lights/projections/sound for student Lab Show productions (typically 2 per semester) as needed. Seek out, develop, recruit, and mentor undergraduate design students. Manage and maintain lighting, sound, and projection equipment. Maintain and adhere to production budget in areas of lighting, sound, and projections. Integrate contemporary theatre professional design practice into student learning by sharing all phases of the design process, from research and concept development through realization.  Assist department chair with yearly Capital Equipment and Capital Improvement requests. Coordinate acquisition and installation of approved items in relevant design areas. Attend all department, theatre, and production meetings. Comply with all Trinity University policies and guidelines. Additional duties as assigned.   APPOINTMENT DETAILS This is a three-year renewable full-time Lecturer of Lighting Design position beginning August 2027, with the possibility of promotion to Senior Lecturer. The salary will be competitive.   QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Terminal Degree in Theatre (MFA) or related area by the time of appointment.    EXPERIENCE Required: Experience teaching or serving as a teaching assistant in an academic environment Experience training and supervising student workers Evidence of experience in lighting design in a variety of theatrical styles Preferred: Experience in projection and/or sound design Experience with QLab, Dante, Shure Workbench, Vectorworks, and Lightwright   PHYSICAL DEMANDS AND WORKING CONDITIONS Work is completed in classroom, office, theater, and studio environments.  Will work under pressure to meet office/project deadlines.  Initial design conversations for Fall 2027 mainstage season will begin in Summer 2027.  Must be able to move about freely. (ladders, lifts, scaffolding, cat walk) Willingness to travel on behalf of Trinity University.  Physical demands are moderate. Most lifting is less than 50 pounds. Occasionally, the incumbent will be required to pick up or deliver information to an off-campus office. ATTENDANCE Train and Supervise student workers during daily shop hours Mon-Fri 2:30-5:30. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants, please apply through Workday using the 'Jobs Hub' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants, when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  File 1: CV File 2: Cover letter  File 3: List of three professional references (we will request recommendation letters for finalists) File 4: Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities.   Candidates may be asked to submit a sample syllabus. CONTACT INFORMATION Dr. Andrew C. Hansen, Search Committee Chair, Department of Human Communication and Theatre, at ahansen1@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Visiting-Assistant-Professor---International-Business_JR101670": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor - International Business",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Business Administration Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description:   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . At the Neidorff School of Business at Trinity University, innovation meets impact. Located in the 7th largest city in the United States, where we’re redefining what it means to deliver a modern business education with bold ideas, ethical leadership, and global perspective coming together. Our AACSB-accredited programs - ranked #1 among undergraduate business schools in Texas and #27 in the nation - empower students to think strategically, lead confidently, and make ethical decisions grounded in data and purpose. Our faculty are mentors, teacher-scholars, and catalysts for transformation. The Neidorff School thrives on collaboration across disciplines, forging powerful connections with humanities, social sciences and STEM disciplines. With cutting-edge facilities, strong industry partnerships, and a student-centered culture that prizes creativity and inclusion, Neidorff is a dynamic hub for shaping the next generation of business leaders who make a difference in the world. Join us, and be part of a school that’s driven by purpose, powered by people, and poised for the future. The Department of Business Administration is one of the three departments housed in the Neidorff School of Business. Home to innovative scholars and industry-connected faculty, the department advances leadership, entrepreneurship, marketing, and global business through collaborative teaching, mentorship, and applied learning. We develop ethical, well-rounded leaders through an interdisciplinary, integrated business education. Majors in the department include business administration, human resources management, international business, and marketing, and houses courses in business law and strategic management. Our students learn business by doing business. They research alongside faculty mentors, intern with local organizations, study abroad, serve the San Antonio community, attend industry conferences, participate in consulting projects, and enter case competitions — all while gaining valuable skills and experience. Both the Department of Business Administration and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teach a total of three courses (9-12 contact hours per semester) in the spring of 2027 to support the curricular needs of the department. Primary teaching responsibility is in the area of undergraduate International Business Strategy, Global Business Culture, and International Business Environment, with a willingness to teach other teaching assignments as needed.   QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: To be considered for this position, applicants should hold a Doctoral Degree (or ABD) in International Business or a closely related field from a program accredited by AACSB EXPERIENCE Required: Evidence of commitment to high quality classroom performance; understanding of the Trinity mission with an interest in interdisciplinary teaching and engagement; and demonstrated collegiality, respect, and empathy for the inclusion of diverse stakeholders. ATTENDANCE This is an in-person teaching position, not online or remote. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants, please apply through Workday using the 'Jobs Hub' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants, when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  Cover Letter including a statement on why the candidate is interested in teaching at Trinity University Curriculum vitae; names and contact information for three professional references (they will only be contacted if you are one of the finalist candidates) Statement of the candidate's teaching philosophy and qualifications and most recent teaching evaluations Other materials of the candidate’s choosing, combined in a pdf file Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. CONTACT INFORMATION Dr. Yi Liu, Associate Professor of HRM, yliu@trinity.edu Dr. Rita Kosnik, Professor of Management and Negotiations, rkosnik@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Visiting-Assistant-Professor---Biology_JR101667": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor - Biology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Full time Department/Office: Biology Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Trinity University seeks applications for a one-year Visiting Assistant Professor (VAP) in the Department of Biology beginning in January 2027. The VAP will teach courses in Ecology and laboratory sections in the introductory biology core sequence.   BACKGROUND Trinity University is a secular, independent, private institution, founded in 1869. Trinity offers high-quality science, liberal arts, and pre-professional programs as represented by the four schools (STEM, Arts & Humanities, Social Science and Civic Engagement, and Business) to approximately 2,500 undergraduate students from the U.S. and more than 45 countries. The attractive campus overlooks downtown San Antonio, a city rich in heritage and ethnic diversity. Additional information can be found on the Resource Guide to Trinity & San Antonio . The Department of Biology is one of eight departments in the D. R. Semmes School of Science, endowed with a gift of $26 million that in part supports student research and faculty development. The D. R. Semmes School of Science is housed in the Center for the Sciences and Innovation , an innovative and collaborative hub for interdisciplinary research and teaching. The Department of Biology (https://trinity.edu/biology) is composed of 16 faculty with diverse research interests and serves majors in Biology, Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Environmental Studies, Global Public Health and Neuroscience. Both the Department of Biology and the University as a whole value and safeguard academic freedom for faculty members in their teaching and scholarship. Trinity is guided by a set of core values and is committed to implementing practices in pursuit of an inclusive campus for all students, faculty, and staff to feel welcomed and engaged in a community of learning.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES The VAP will teach an ecology course with two lab sections spring 2027 and introductory laboratory lab sections Fall 2027. The VAP will be responsible for delivering lectures, teaching lab sections, assigning grades, maintaining office hours, adherence to instructional policies as described in the Faculty Handbook, and making student accommodations as determined by the Office of Student Accessibility Services (the equivalent of six courses over Spring 2027 and Fall 2027 terms).   APPOINTMENT DETAILS The position will be a two-semester appointment, beginning in the spring semester, January 2027, and continuing through the Fall 2027 semester.   QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: PhD in some area of biological sciences Preferred: Preference will be given to candidates with a PhD in some area of ecology or evolution EXPERIENCE Preferred: Previous teaching experience at the undergraduate level KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES Mastery of the relevant subject matter Ability to deliver engaging lectures, discussions, or in-class activities Ability to design rigorous and meaningful assignments Commitment to the liberal arts mission of Trinity University   PHYSICAL DEMANDS AND WORKING CONDITIONS Work is completed in the office, classroom and in teaching laboratory spaces. Must be able to move about freely. Willingness to travel on behalf of Trinity University. Physical demands are light; all lifting is less than 50 pounds. ATTENDANCE Maintaining and satisfying minimum attendance requirements is an essential function of this position. This job classification may not include “light duty” work or allow unpredictable or unrestricted absences. On-site attendance required. HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants, please apply through Workday using the 'Jobs Hub' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants, when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following as seen below.  File 1: Cover letter File 2: Current curriculum vitae File 3: Unofficial transcripts from postsecondary institutions attended File 4: A teaching philosophy statement, including evidence of implementation of this philosophy in past teaching and/or training efforts File 5: Intentional inclusion statement - see guidance below Intentional inclusion statement - Intentional Inclusion is a core value at Trinity University. Please provide a brief (1-2 page) reflection on the ways that you might contribute to cultivating an environment where people feel welcomed, respected, and empowered to reach their full potential. Provide examples of how that relates to your teaching, research/scholarship and other professional activities. CONTACT INFORMATION Sandra Miller SANDRA.MILLER@trinity.edu Frank Healy fhealy@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Groundskeeper-_JR101663": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper-",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nJob Family Group: Staff Department/Office: Groundskeeping Time Type: Full time Compensation: $17.00-$21.17 Please note, starting salaries are based on factors including internal equity, relevant experience, and education. Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Performs relatively simple, semi-skilled duties in the construction, repair, and maintenance of the university grounds and/or property. This is a temporary position intended to support seasonal and operational grounds maintenance needs. Temporary employees may be considered for future regular employment opportunities based on departmental needs, qualifications, and performance.   JOB DUTIES Operates commercial grounds maintenance equipment and vehicles, including mowers, string trimmers, backpack blowers, and utility vehicles, to perform campus landscape maintenance safely and efficiently. Picks up campus litter and empties trash receptacles. Occasionally performs general maintenance such as landscape installations, basic constructions, and power washing of university property, etc. Mulches landscape beds and assists with planting, watering, weeding, and maintaining trees, shrubs, flowers, turf, and groundcover. Cleans parking garages and parking lots.  Assists with emergency response activities including storm debris removal, unclogging drainage systems, ice mitigation, and cleanup following severe weather events.    May assist other Grounds, Facilities Operations, or University departments with routine operational support activities including event setup and breakdown, furniture moving, warehouse support, material handling, and related tasks. ADDITIONAL DUTIES Operates, cleans, inspects, and performs preventive maintenance on grounds equipment and vehicles, including routine greasing and fluid checks. Follows departmental and University safety procedures and properly utilizes required personal protective equipment (PPE) while performing assigned duties. Reports unsafe conditions, equipment damage, accidents, and campus property concerns to supervisors in a timely manner. Collaborates effectively with colleagues, demonstrating teamwork, professionalism, integrity, and effective problem-solving during routine and emergency situations. Maintaining satisfactory attendance and availability is an essential function of this position, including working all scheduled hours as assigned by the supervisor, scheduled or emergency overtime, and reporting to work during emergency situations, adverse weather conditions, or University closures when designated as essential personnel. Complies with University policies, procedures, and guidelines and performs other duties as assigned. EDUCATION Required: Less than High School Diploma. Preferred: High School Diploma or GED. EXPERIENCE Required: None. Preferred: One year of general lawn care experience. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES Preferred: Demonstrates attention to detail and accuracy in all work performed. Ability to communicate effectively with supervisors, coworkers, and members of the campus community; Spanish-English bilingual skills preferred. Works effectively and cooperatively as part of a team in support of departmental and University goals and providing excellent service to the campus community. Ability to understand and follow verbal and written instructions. Maintains reliable attendance and punctuality and demonstrates a strong commitment to assigned responsibilities. LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS Required: ●        Must possess a valid Texas Driver’s License and have a good driving history.  ●        May not have more than two moving violations in a three-year period. OTHER REQUIREMENTS ●        All jobs require a current Criminal Background Check (CBC). ●        Must be able to pass a physical examination. SUPERVISORY RESPONSIBILITIES No supervisory responsibility."
    },
    "https://trinity.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Trinity_University/job/Trinity-University-Campus/Part-Time-Faculty---Finance_JR101674": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - Finance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nJob Family Group: Faculty Time Type: Part time Department/Office: Finance and Business Analytics Exemption Status: Exempt Job Description: Trinity University is seeking a part-time faculty member for the Spring 2027 semester to teach one face-to-face section of FNCE 3290 - Studies in Finance (Effective Visualizations for Business) on the university’s San Antonio, Texas campus. The successful applicant must possess a Master’s degree or higher and at least three years of practical business experience designing and building visualizations to assist organizational decision making.   DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Typical responsibilities of part-time faculty members include but are not limited to: Developing the course syllabus and ensuring that the syllabus meets departmental and University standards Designing and delivering lectures, discussions, in-class activities, and assignments Grading assigned papers, quizzes, and exams Assigning early, midterm, and final grades in compliance with University deadlines Maintaining office hours as directed by the department chair or program director Making student accommodations as determined by the Office of Student Accessibility Services Adhering to all instructional policies as published in the Faculty Handbook and the Courses of Study Bulletin QUALIFICATIONS EDUCATION Required: Masters of Science or higher EXPERIENCE Required: At least three years of practical business experience designing and building visualizations to support organizational decision making LICENSES/CERTIFICATIONS Preferred: Tableau Certified Data Analyst Certification KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ABILITIES The ideal candidate will demonstrate: Mastery of the relevant subject matter Ability to deliver engaging lectures, discussions, or in-class activities Ability to design rigorous and meaningful assignments Commitment to the liberal arts mission of Trinity University HOW TO APPLY For internal applicants, please apply through Workday using the 'Jobs Hub' application, see instructions provided here . For external applicants, when starting the application, select \"Apply Manually.” Only complete fields with a red asterisk, which are required. Please create your Workday application account using a personal email address , as you will need to maintain access to this account throughout the pre-hire process, if selected.  For all applicants, you do not need to complete the “(Work) Experience,” “Education,” “Certifications,” or “Languages” fields - these details will be provided in your curriculum vitae. In the “Application Documents” field, please upload the following, each as a separate pdf file [note: a maximum of five (5) files can be uploaded]: Cover letter  Curriculum vitae or resume  Names and contact information for three professional references CONTACT INFORMATION Diana K. Young; dyoung1@trinity.edu Please ensure that all required documents are uploaded prior to submitting an application. If you have revisions needed to an application already submitted or need help submitting an application, please contact Human Resources at 210-999-7507 or email humanresources@trinity.edu ."
    }
  },
